841":2163,"2842":2481,"2843":796,"2844":847,"2845":2220,"2846":1902,"2847":2050,"2848":2970,"2849":1306,"2850":2798,"2851":165,"2852":500,"2853":512,"2854":374,"2855":564,"2856":515,"2857":729,"2858":727,"2859":392,"2860":731,"2861":728,"2862":375,"2863":732,"2864":730,"2865":592,"2866":734,"2867":733,"2868":331,"2869":228,"2870":226,"2871":474,"2872":227,"2873":225,"2874":1799,"2875":335,"2876":76,"2877":491,"2878":885,"2879":886,"2880":1095,"2881":689,"2882":290,"2883":489,"2884":1282,"2885":2586,"2886":1462,"2887":490,"2888":2358,"2889":2672,"2890":160,"2891":2691,"2892":977,"2893":2758,"2894":888,"2895":1708,"2896":1448,"2897":2164,"2898":914,"2899":1172,"2900":1262,"2901":1204,"2902":1787,"2903":1786,"2904":1903,"2905":1599,"2906":2051,"2907":2971,"2908":561,"2909":2497,"2910":2552,"2911":855,"2912":913,"2913":756,"2914":1929,"2915":139,"2916":323,"2917":927,"2918":2526,"2919":2678,"2920":571,"2921":2566,"2922":705,"2923":2696,"2924":570,"2925":706,"2926":420,"2927":654,"2928":2633,"2929":152,"2930":149,"2931":1359,"2932":1358,"2933":1738,"2934":463,"2935":560,"2936":563,"2937":557,"2938":343,"2939":559,"2940":562,"2941":558,"2942":376,"2943":1739,"2944":987,"2945":137,"2946":2185,"2947":108,"2948":2323,"2949":1020,"2950":971,"2951":311,"2952":1573,"2953":778,"2954":1089,"2955":3015,"2956":2694,"2957":925,"2958":2695,"2959":2576,"2960":1320,"2961":1733,"2962":1734,"2963":2972,"2964":1131,"2965":830,"2966":917,"2967":760,"2968":1568,"2969":2322,"2970":1709,"2971":1449,"2972":2165,"2973":2228,"2974":1288,"2975":1904,"2976":2052,"2977":2973,"2978":378,"2979":377,"2980":1558,"2981":410,"2982":694,"2983":308,"2984":1109,"2985":2287,"2986":2286,"2987":985,"2988":1710,"2989":1450,"2990":2166,"2991":2760,"2992":358,"2993":2105,"2994":1905,"2995":504,"2996":501,"2997":2053,"2998":2974,"2999":502,"3000":503,"3001":2531,"3002":2530,"3003":926,"3004":309,"3005":582,"3006":328,"3007":767,"3008":639,"3009":595,"3010":505,"3011":974,"3012":1309,"3013":98,"3014":2800,"3015":2532,"3016":2644,"3017":2614,"3018":2336,"3019":918,"3020":1711,"3021":1451,"3022":2167,"3023":2823,"3024":2060,"3025":1906,"3026":2054,"3027":2975,"3028":541,"3029":2796,"3030":484,"3031":485,"3032":2264,"3033":2265,"3034":613,"3035":100,"3036":542,"3037":161,"3038":1202,"3039":836,"3040":1005},"orderedDocs":[[1684,"— (the avatar)"],[124,"\"Add to collection\" opens `AddToCollectionPanel` in a popover anchored to the button, instead of the dialog:"],[122,"\"Add to collection\" reuses the same `AddToCollection`\ncallable a single-document flow would call, passed the whole `multiSelection` array at once — the dialog\nalready accepts one article or an array. If every selected document ends up removed from the target collection\n(the dialog resolves `\"dialog-no\"`), the toolbar clears the selection on the assumption the user is done with\nit."],[126,"\"New collection\" opens a name field at the top of the list, which stays on screen, dimmed (a taken name is\nrefused as it is typed; Escape goes back to the list, a second Escape closes the panel). The collection is\ncreated empty and appears at the top of the list, highlighted, with the focus on its `+`;"],[2728,"(A dialog with no `DialogTitle` has no accessible name whichever header it uses: project a titled\n`DialogContent` rather than relying on the default one.)"],[6,"@sinequa/atomic-angular"],[36,"@sinequa/galactik"],[659,"**`(rangeSelected)` only reports a range — it does not zoom anything on its own.** If nothing in the host reacts\nto it, dragging paints a selection rectangle that then disappears and nothing else happens; the component is\nworking as designed. Keep a stack of ranges in the host: a drag pushes one (zoom in), a control pops it (zoom\nout)."],[608,"**`400`, `403`, `500`, `503`** — the only statuses that produce a toast (`404`, `429`, `502`, `504` stay silent), shown as `${statusText}: ${errorCodeText} — ${errorMessage}`, with two\nexceptions: a `400` from `api/v2/user-profile` (handled by the caller instead) and any status from\n`api/v1/preview` (preview failures are shown in the preview surface itself, not as a toast)."],[607,"**`401` during an active user-override**, with a specific server error code — shown once as \"Cannot override\nuser: Unauthorized access\", then a request-scoped flag suppresses a repeat toast for the same request context."],[2488,"**`aria-keyshortcuts`** on the row, set from `keys`;"],[1108,"**`bootstrapApp()` and `ApplicationService.initialize()` are application-level.** They sign the user in on the\nroot client and, with `createRoutes`, rewrite the root router configuration. Call them once, for the root; do not\ncall them again from a scope. To load the second backend's configuration, principal and settings into the scope's\nstores, call `initializeStores({ syncUrl: false })` on the scope's own `ApplicationService` instance."],[2753,"**`topLayer`** makes the panel a manual popover (`popover=\"manual\"` and `showPopover()`): it goes above a `<dialog>`\nand outside the clipping of every ancestor, and is hidden again when it goes away. Without it, a modal dialog clips what\noverflows its own box."],[764,"**1. Install the peer dependency.**"],[765,"**2. Import the stylesheet once**, from the application's global stylesheet (not from a component: at 26 kB\nit would break the default `anyComponentStyle` budget)."],[565,"**A change that cannot be saved** is never left on screen: the switch goes back, the reorder is undone, the deleted row returns, and the reason is shown in a card above the list. It is not a toast — the list lives in popovers and modal dialogs, which are in the top layer, above any toast."],[2672,"**A direction that is not enabled does not move at all** — the row does not give way to the left if only `startEnabled`\nis set."],[478,"**A long name** takes two lines, then is cut. A tooltip with the whole name shows only when the name is actually\ncut, and the full name shows while the name has the keyboard focus."],[2490,"**a second line in the row** (only when `description` is set), shown only while the row has *keyboard* focus (`:focus-visible`): the same\nsentence with the key set apart as a keycap. It is hidden otherwise — the mouse has the click, and showing it\non hover would move the rows under the pointer. The row grows by that one line, and its trailing tag and action\nstay vertically centred on it. The rows below move down by that height while the row is focused, and back up\nwhen the focus leaves: reserving the space, overlaying the line and animating the height were tried and look\nworse, so the shift is accepted."],[2489,"**a sentence** (`description`), rendered visually hidden and linked to the row by `aria-describedby`, so a\nscreen reader reads it when the row takes focus. `aria-keyshortcuts` alone is not reliably read: it depends on\nthe reader and its settings, and is then the raw key name, in English;"],[1968,"**A Tailwind build that scans the library**, as for the Angular components."],[2670,"**A vertical drag is the browser's.** The host sets `touch-action: pan-y`, so a list still scrolls, and a gesture that\nstarts vertical is dropped for good."],[959,"**All** shows the facets of the search, as `<aside-filters>` does (the `position` and `hidden` rules of the `filters` configuration).\nThe field above narrows them by name; with no match, a message replaces them. Under the field, a bar holds the count of documents\nand **Save as Quick Filter**, which opens a card under the button asking for a name and saves the filters applied at that moment.\nThe button is disabled while no filter is applied. `Escape` closes the card only, and the focus goes back to the button."],[2367,"**Backspace** in an empty field removes the last tag. The remove button of a tag removes that one."],[1333,"**Bookmark** (`<bookmark-button>`), when there is an `article`. It is a stateful button of its own, so it always stays\nin the row, and it counts in `maxInline`."],[642,"**Clickable axis labels, reproduced on canvas.** Chart.js's own `options.onClick` never fires on an axis\nlabel — it only fires inside the chart area, and labels sit outside it by construction. `createAxisClickPlugin()`\ndoes its own hit test against the scale's box instead, walking `getPixelForTick()` rather than inverting\n`getValueForPixel()` (which assumes uniform spacing and breaks under `offset`/`autoSkip`, both of which this\ncomponent sets)."],[757,"**Controlled mode**: it is emitted through `(aggregationOverridesChange)`, right before `(filtersChange)`, on the\nsame occasions. Assign it to the query you fetch with."],[1334,"**Copy link**, when the article has an `http:` or `https:` `url1`. It copies the link, shows a check for two seconds\nand announces \"Link copied to clipboard\"."],[310,"**Credentials mode, expired password**: when the partition is editable and the password is expired, the\nguard redirects to `/login?mode=changepassword&alert=passwordExpired&username=…`."],[756,"**Default mode**: the component writes the override to `QueryParamsStore` itself — not to the URL, since a\ncommitted range is self-sufficient; only the masked aggregation values are lost after a page reload until the\nnext apply — and removes it when the filter is cleared."],[1741,"**Default value**"],[563,"**Deleting** hides the row and offers \"Undo\" for six seconds; only then is `UserSettingsStore.deleteAlert`\ncalled. A second deletion, or the list going away (a popover closing), makes the pending one final."],[1688,"**Empty or missing `data`**: all eight are shown."],[482,"**Empty states** all show an icon on a colored tile with a message: no collection yet, no collection matching the\nfilter, an empty collection."],[2031,"**Every workspace is offered**, the ones shared read-only too — mentioning one only reads it. A `predicate` narrows that."],[1335,"**Expand**, unless `canExpand` is `false`. It turns into **Back to the page** while `expanded` is `true`; the\ntwo states have their own icons (`ExpandIcon` and `CollapseIcon` of `@sinequa/galactik`)."],[2099,"**Groups are never offered** in the search: the API documents `owners` and `readers` as *user* ids, so staging a\ngroup would look like it worked and grant nobody anything."],[279,"**Injected principals** are always in the emitted lists — see below."],[2751,"**It is hidden until placed.** The panel is `visibility: hidden` until its first position is computed, so it never\npaints for a frame in the corner of the page."],[2752,"**It may come and go.** `panel` and `anchor` are signal reads: the panel is positioned each time it appears, and the\n`autoUpdate` of the previous one is stopped."],[1107,"**Keep the scopes from fighting over the URL.** `QueryParamsStore` writes `q`, `f`, `p`, `s`, `t`, `b` and `n` into\nthe one browser URL, and there is a single URL for both backends. In the scope that must not own it, pass\n`{ syncUrl: false }` to the store mutations (`patch(params, audit, { syncUrl: false })`, `updateFilter`,\n`removeFilterByName`, `clearFilters`…) and use `<applied-filters [syncUrl]=\"false\">` (and `[syncUrl]=\"false\"` on\nthe facets): the scope's state is still updated, the URL is left alone."],[2369,"**Leaving the field** adds what is typed (`addOnBlur`, on by default), so a Save button never loses the last word."],[825,"**Missing** — no entry in the store matching `name`/`column`, or an empty `[aggregation]` in controlled mode"],[2030,"**New workspace** opens the dialog of the workspaces page. The workspace it makes is ticked and shown: the category goes back\nto \"all\" and the search is cleared, so it is not hidden."],[2509,"**No editable content in a row.** A `ListItem` is a listbox `option`: ARIA forbids an interactive element inside\nit, and the row takes `Delete` (and `Backspace&#x60; on a Mac) for its action whatever has the focus. A row that holds a\ntext field, a select or any other control is not a list row — use &#x2A;*`RowGrid`** instead."],[309,"**Not authenticated** (non-SSO): redirect to `loginPath` with the original URL as `returnUrl`."],[1723,"**Not supported yet:**"],[2503,"**One action per row.** `ListItem` looks for a single `ListItemAction` (the first it projects) and gives it the key.\nThat is a property of the component, not an omission of this page."],[1715,"**One row per leaf**, named after its path: `{ \"workingHours\": { \"start\": \"9:00\" } }` gives `workingHours › start`. Objects nest: `hours › extra › lunch`."],[2669,"**Only a finger or a pen** drives it (`pointerType` other than `mouse`): with a mouse the row keeps its hover actions,\nand selecting text by dragging is untouched."],[1691,"**Order**: rows follow the order of the table above, whatever the order of your list."],[2368,"**Pasting** `a, b, c` (or one tag per line) adds three tags."],[481,"**Previewing** a document: a click on a document opens `<preview-content>` on the right of the list, in a\nresizable panel. The user drags the handle (or uses the arrow keys on it) to give the preview more or less room;\nthe list never goes under a third of the width, and the chosen layout is remembered in `localStorage` under\n`collection-detail`. The preview closes with its cross, with the Escape key (not while the focus is inside the\ndocument itself, an iframe, which keeps the key), or when its document is taken out of the collection. When a\npreview opens, the list slides from the center to the left, and back to the center when it closes, unless the user\nasked for reduced motion. On a detail narrower than 640px (the width of the component, not of the window) there is\nno room for both: the preview replaces the list. The preview shows the document alone: its action bar (search,\nzoom, pages) is hidden with `showActions`."],[1249,"**Proactive.** The token `@sinequa/atomic` stores — the `csrfToken` of the sign-in and challenge responses, and the\n`sinequa-jwt-refresh` header — looks like `<opaque token>|<epoch in seconds>`, and the suffix is the expiry of the\nsession. It is the only expiry date a browser can read, since the session cookie is `HttpOnly`. The provider reads it\nat start-up and on every `tokenRefreshed`, and schedules the warning `warnBeforeMs` before it. The warning is raised\nonce: it is armed again only when a renewal takes the expiry back out of that window."],[960,"**Quick Filters** is the list of the Quick filters pill, inline (`<quick-filters-list>`), one line each:"],[1250,"**Reactive.** A `401` that reaches its caller on a session that had been established means it is gone, whatever the\nsign-in, and raises the error toast once. The provider follows the client's `sessionLost` event (`@sinequa/atomic`\n2.8 or later), not `unauthorized`: `unauthorized` fires as soon as the `401` is read, before the recovery that\n`bootstrapApp` installs gets its chance, so a session it re-establishes silently (single sign-on) would be announced\nlost. The client already leaves out the `401`s that are answers rather than expiries — the sign-in, the start-up\nprobe, audit events — and the requests trailing behind a logout. Requests sent with `HttpClient`, which the client\nnever sees, are covered by `errorInterceptorFn` when its own recovery gives up. A `401`\nwhile a user is impersonated is left to its own message in `provideAtomicNotifications()`."],[1712,"**Read-only tags** (a galactik `TagInput`), and an edit button that opens the array as **indented JSON** in a text area."],[1938,"**Regular query** — uses the query configuration's `sortingChoices` directly."],[2098,"**Removing asks first**, inline in the row."],[480,"**Renaming** a collection: the pencil next to its name turns the name into a field. Enter or the check saves\n(`UserSettingsStore.updateBasket`), Escape or the cross gives up. A name another collection already has is refused\nas it is typed, however it is cased, and so is an empty one. The detail stays open on the renamed collection."],[562,"**Reordering** uses the browser's native drag and drop (`draggable`, no `@angular/cdk`) — and Alt +\n↑ / ↓ on a focused row, which announces the new position. The order is shown at once and\nsaved with `UserSettingsStore.updateAlerts`; if saving fails, the previous order comes back. A touch screen\nthat does not start a native drag has no way to reorder."],[827,"**Resolves to zero items**"],[1739,"**Saved**"],[2029,"**Select all** works on what is displayed — the current category and search — and unticks them all when they already are.\nWhat the search hides keeps its tick. It shows a mixed state when only some of the displayed rows are ticked."],[2325,"**Single vs. multiple** only changes the shape of `value` and whether the popup closes on pick: single mode\n(default) is `TOption | null` and closes the popup once a choice is made; `multiple` makes `value` an array,\nkeeps the popup open across picks, and shows a check mark via the option template's `selected` context."],[1689,"**Spelling**: values are matched exactly, case included — `\"FullName\"` matches nothing."],[308,"**SSO mode** (`authMode.kind === \"sso\"`): the browser/proxy carries the auth, so an unauthenticated state is\nlet through — the session is established out of band."],[1937,"**Tab search** — extracts sorting choices from the specific tab's own configuration (`tab.sortingChoices`),\nfalling back to the query's if the tab defines none."],[277,"**The creator** (`creator`, optional) comes first with the Owner badge and has no menu and no remove button."],[479,"**The detail** loads the documents of the collection through a query scoped by `basket` (100 at most): the user's\nsettings only keep their ids. Taking a document out writes the store, and the list follows without another\nrequest."],[2004,"**The detail** reads the workspace and its documents, with search, multi-selection, upload, edit, share and delete.\nOnly someone who can write to the workspace sees the edit, share, delete, upload and select controls."],[477,"**The filter** matches names ignoring case and accents, and marks the match in the name."],[625,"**The host must have a resolved height** — Chart.js measures the canvas' parent box, and a blank chart is\nalmost always a container with no height (`class=\"h-64\"` above)."],[2671,"**The layers are as wide as the space the drag frees**, pinned to their edge: they never sit behind the row, so the\nrow needs no opaque background of its own to hide them."],[2003,"**The list** refreshes each time it is shown — workspaces created by others since the last time appear, and\nthe document counts match the server. The list already on screen stays until the answer lands, and a failure leaves\nit untouched behind an inline error. It filters by tab (all, editor, reader) and by search, and offers multi-selection\nand deletion for the workspaces the user can write to."],[475,"**The list** shows a card per collection (a row in list mode) with its name, the number of documents, an **Apply**\nbutton and a delete cross. A click on the card, or on its name, opens the detail. The cross shows on hover; on a\ntouch screen a row is deleted by dragging it to the left, and the cross stays in the page for screen readers."],[2876,"**The page scroll is locked while a sheet is open** — the one deliberate divergence from `Dialog`. A dialog\ncovers a small part of the viewport and locks nothing; a sheet covers a whole edge of it, so a wheel or touch\ndrag scrolling the page behind it would be disorienting. The lock is counted (`openSheets`), so stacked sheets\nrestore the previous value only once, at the last close — and it compensates for the scrollbar it removes with\nan equal `padding-right`, so a consumer's width-based media query (a `BreakpointObserverService` mobile/desktop\nsplit, say) never observes a spurious viewport-width change from opening one."],[2750,"**The panel must be `position: fixed`**, as a class on it: it is then never in flow, not even on the frame it is created.\nFloating UI answers in viewport coordinates; without `fixed` they would be relative to the offset parent and the panel\nwould drift by the page's scroll."],[1969,"**The prebuilt stylesheet**, `workspace-elements/styles/lexiq-workspaces.css`, for a page without a Tailwind build.\nIt holds the galactik tokens as a *fallback* (a host that already defines them keeps its own), a small reset limited\nto the elements and the dialogs they open, and exactly the utilities the components use — unlayered, so a class beats\nthe page's own `h3 { … }` or `p { … }`. The dark tokens apply under a `.dark` class: `theme=\"dark\"` on\n`<lexiq-workspaces>` puts it on the element."],[278,"**The public value** (`publicValue`, `*` by default) and the value of each custom switch are reader values:\nthe switch adds or removes them, and they never show up as rows."],[2673,"**The release does not click.** The click the browser fires under the finger after a drag is swallowed, so a swipe never\nopens the row it deletes."],[1106,"**The root must provide `provideRouter()` and `provideTransloco()`.** The scoped `QueryParamsStore`,\n`ApplicationService` and `QueryService` inject the router, the activated route and `TranslocoService` from the\nroot; without them the scope fails with a `NullInjectorError`."],[2365,"**The separator** (`,` by default; `separator=\",;\"` gives several) and **Enter** add the tag being typed. Enter never\nsubmits the surrounding form."],[564,"**The switch** writes `active` through `UserSettingsStore.updateAlert`, without opening the dialog."],[476,"**The width.** The manager fills its parent. The list views (the grid, the list, their header and toolbar) are\ncentered and stop at `max-w-4xl` (56rem), however wide the parent is: a row stretched over a very wide screen puts\nthe name and its delete cross a screen apart. The detail caps and centers only its head (back, name, count, apply) and its rows: the head stays in place when a\npreview opens, only the list slides, and the preview can use the room. The scrollbar\nof the list stays at the edge of the parent. The Bookmarks tab follows the detail: its rows stop at `max-w-4xl`, centered when alone and moved to the\nleft, with the same slide, when a preview opens, so that the preview can use the room."],[2705,"**Three open modes**, all on the same template reference:"],[2097,"**Two rows never get a role menu**: the creator's, which the API treats as immutable, and your own — an owner who\ndemotes or removes themselves loses the workspace with no way back."],[1690,"**Unknown values are ignored**, without a warning. A list made only of unknown values therefore shows no\nstandard field at all (it does not fall back to \"all of them\")."],[1647,"**What the form shows is not decided by the code but by the administrator**, in the application's custom JSON\n(`general.features.userProfile`). Users change *values*; they never change the *structure*. This page covers both\nsides: how the form works, and how to write that configuration — including what is read-only."],[2028,"**Where the ticks live.** Without `selectedIds` they are in `WorkspacesStore`, so every menu on screen shows the same ones. With\n`selectedIds` they are yours: the store is left alone, and you hear each change from `selectionChanged` and feed it back."],[2032,"**With content inside**, the ready-made menu is not drawn: the element is only the provider of the state."],[1743,"*Enter to save · Escape to cancel*"],[774,"`--bg-neutral-white` / `--font-neutral-base`"],[777,"`--bg-primary-base` / `--font-neutral-white`"],[783,"`--bg-primary-lighter` / `--bg-primary-light`"],[780,"`--bg-primary-lighter` / `--font-primary-base`"],[786,"`--bg-primary-lighter` / `--font-primary-base`"],[789,"`--font-neutral-disabled`"],[3013,"`--sidebar-width`/`--sidebar-width-icon` must be declared on `SidebarComponent`'s own host (an ancestor of\nthe reserved-width slot), never only on the `<aside>` it visually applies to — CSS custom properties only\never inherit downward, and the slot `<div>` is `<aside>`'s **parent**. Declaring a variable only on a\ndescendant leaves an ancestor's own `var()` reference unresolved. The general rule: before reading a custom\nproperty through `var()` on element A, make sure it is declared on A itself or one of A's actual ancestors."],[792,"`--stroke-focus`"],[3035,"`--tab-max-width` (fallback `100%`) caps a tab's label width on any ancestor; `noTruncate` exempts a single\ntab from it."],[772,"`--vc-bg` / `--vc-color`"],[787,"`--vc-date-disabled-color`"],[781,"`--vc-date-hover-bg` / `--vc-date-hover-edge-bg`"],[778,"`--vc-date-range-middle-bg` / `--vc-date-range-middle-color`"],[775,"`--vc-date-selected-bg` / `--vc-date-selected-color`"],[784,"`--vc-date-today-bg` / `--vc-date-today-color`"],[790,"`--vc-focus-outline-color`"],[2947,"`.tooltip` class styled by the application"],[237,"`\"\"`"],[225,"`\"/search\"`"],[1036,"`\"bottom-start\"`"],[208,"`\"card\" \\| \"inline\"`"],[209,"`\"card\"`"],[1031,"`\"default\" \\| \"pill\"`"],[201,"`\"medium\"`"],[1696,"`\"nickname\"`"],[1032,"`\"pill\"`"],[1451,"`\"redirect\"` only navigates when the article has an `id`. Without one, it falls back to `\"replace\"` — the same\nbehavior as if no strategy were set at all. Make sure the `article` passed in actually carries an `id` before\nrelying on the redirect."],[1028,"`\"sage\"`"],[200,"`\"small\" \\| \"medium\" \\| \"large\" \\| \"hero\"`"],[680,"`(axisSelected)` reports the value **behind** the label, not the label text — under the `\"cross\"` encoding the\ntwo differ, so always read `.value` when building a filter, never `.label`."],[865,"`(changed)` fires on any mutation — a removal or a full clear alike — so a host that only needs to re-run its\nquery can bind that one output and ignore the more precise `(removed)`/`(cleared)`."],[2702,"`(closed)=\"call.end($event === 'dialog-confirm')\"` resolves the promise however the dialog closes — clicking\nConfirm, clicking Cancel, or pressing `Escape` all reach the same handler, with no dedicated \"Escape\" branch\nto write."],[232,"`(text: string) => SearchBoxSuggestion[] \\| Promise<SearchBoxSuggestion[]>`"],[900,"`[aggregations]` resolves each entry against the `AggregationsStore` **first by name, then by column** —\nit does not create an aggregation from nothing. If the store has no entry matching either, that name is\nsilently dropped from the rendered list, not shown as an error. Confirm the aggregation is actually present in\nthe current query's result — `[aggregations]` filters an existing list, it does not fetch one."],[1284,"`[childMarker]` marks a projected `<ng-template>` and exposes its `TemplateRef` publicly, so the parent\ncomponent that receives it (typically through `contentChildren`) can render it wherever and however many times\nit needs to — a `@for` loop being the common case."],[2789,"`[disabled]=\"true\"` excludes an item from keyboard navigation and selection. `.heading` spans and `role=\"group\"`\nwrappers are not focusable and are skipped by the roving navigation."],[2374,"`[formField]` rewrites the `id` of the inner input, and the host is a group, not a control. Name the field with\n`aria-label` (and show the visible label next to it), as the examples do."],[1619,"`[keyShortcut]` mirrors this as inputs: `keyShortcut` (the combo, required), `shortcutLabelKey`,\n`shortcutCategoryKey`, `shortcutDisabled` (unregisters while `true`), `shortcutHidden`, `shortcutGlobal`."],[1613,"`[keyShortcut]` registers the combination, writes `aria-keyshortcuts` on the host so assistive technology\nannounces it, and activates the host when the combination fires — the same `(click)` serves the mouse, the\nTab-and-Enter path, and the shortcut, in one code path. The registration disappears with the element, which is\nwhat gives a page-scoped shortcut with nothing to clean up by hand."],[2794,"`[menuTrigger]` only sets `popovertarget` — the native browser auto-invoke for that attribute only applies to\n`<button>`, so a plain element needs to open the menu imperatively, e.g. on `contextmenu`."],[2785,"`[menuTrigger]=\"menu\"` wires `popovertarget`, `aria-haspopup=\"menu\"` and a reactive `aria-expanded` onto the\nbutton — no manual `id` to invent or keep in sync. See Pitfalls for the one thing this example\nwould silently break without: the `#menu=\"menu\"` export form."],[1314,"`[openArticleOnCtrlEnter]` opens an article's preview in a new browser tab when the user presses **Ctrl+Enter**\nwhile the host element is focused — the same \"open in background tab\" gesture browsers already give search\nresult links."],[1448,"`[selectArticle]` selects an article when its host element is clicked or receives an Enter keypress, using one\nof three strategies."],[1414,"`[slot='actions']`"],[1412,"`[slot='icon']`"],[2945,"`[strategy]`"],[1523,"`[themeProvider]` reads a scope's current theme and dark-mode state from `ThemeStore` and writes\nthe matching CSS variables directly onto the element it is bound to, via `element.style.setProperty`. Every\ndescendant inherits those variables through normal CSS cascade — including another `[themeProvider]` further\ndown, which is what makes nesting themes just a matter of choosing distinct scope names."],[2915,"`[tooltipOptions]` takes the same settings as the inputs. An input set on its own wins over the object."],[1698,"`{ \"team\": \"Design\" }`"],[1214,"`{{ value | fileSize }}` alone stringifies the returned `{ key, value }` object. Read `.value` and `.key`\nseparately — see the recipe above — and translate `.key` through your application's own translation pipe."],[2530,"`@angular/aria` has no feed primitive, so the keyboard lives in the component: the articles are a roving tab\nstop (`tabindex` 0 on one, -1 on the others), and `↑`/`↓`, `PageUp`/`PageDown`, `Home` and `End` move from one\narticle to the next without wrapping. `Enter` on the article itself emits `rowActivate`. `Tab` walks the\ncontrols inside the article as in any page, and keys pressed on those controls keep their own meaning."],[2203,"`@angular/aria`'s Combobox opens the popover on every keystroke, long before the debounce elapses and the fetch\nstarts. `Autocomplete` takes the popup back (`expanded` follows its own \"a search is on screen\" state, put back\nin a microtask), so the row only ever appears once a search has genuinely come back empty."],[1964,"`@sinequa/atomic-angular/workspace-elements` wraps the components of `document-workspaces` with `@angular/elements`:\n`<lexiq-workspaces>`, `<lexiq-workspace-picker>` and `<lexiq-workspace-mention-list>`. An Angular application should use\nthe components directly; the elements are for hosts that have no Angular component to put them in."],[10,"`@sinequa/atomic-angular` composes Angular search features — sign-in, filtering and aggregations, document\npreview, collections, saved searches, and more — on top of two lower-level libraries: `@sinequa/atomic` (the\nframework-agnostic client that talks to a Sinequa server) and `@sinequa/galactik` (the presentation components\nthis library's UI is built from). It is the only layer in this stack allowed to depend on both a presentation\nlibrary and the backend client at once."],[24,"`@sinequa/atomic-angular` composes two lower-level libraries — `@sinequa/galactik` (presentation) and\n`@sinequa/atomic` (the framework-agnostic client). Both are peer dependencies, alongside `@angular/aria`."],[12,"`@sinequa/atomic-angular` never renders raw presentation markup of its own where an equivalent already exists\nin `@sinequa/galactik` — new code here composes `galactik` components rather than reinventing them. It no\nlonger depends on `@sinequa/ui`, the older presentation library `galactik` replaces; see this repository's\n`ARCHITECTURE.md` for the state of that migration."],[1985,"`@sinequa/atomic` 2.9 or later, which carries the Agent Workspace API the store calls (`listWorkspaces`,\n`createWorkspace`, `addWorkspaceDocuments`…), a backend that exposes `api/v2/agent-workspace/*`, and a configured\nclient (`setGlobalConfig`, or the application's own bootstrap)."],[2930,"`@sinequa/galactik`"],[40,"`@sinequa/galactik` is a library of Angular presentation components: buttons, form controls, overlays\n(dialogs, menus, popovers), navigation (tabs, sidebar) and display primitives (badges, tags, avatars). It has\nno knowledge of search, of a Sinequa backend, or of any business concept — every component takes plain inputs\nand emits plain outputs, and every visual choice comes from a fixed set of design tokens."],[42,"`@sinequa/galactik` never imports `@sinequa/ui` (the library it replaces, still named \"atomic-ui\" in some\nplaces) and never imports `@sinequa/atomic-angular` (the business layer that composes search features on top\nof both). If you are looking for a search results list, a query builder, or anything that talks to a Sinequa\nserver, that lives in `@sinequa/atomic-angular` instead — this library only supplies the visual building\nblocks it is composed from."],[2929,"`@sinequa/ui`"],[2518,"`@sinequa/ui` (atomic-ui) also exports a `ListItemComponent`, but as a thinner styling-only directive\n(variants `default`/`destructive`/`primary`/`link`/`none`) with no `List` container, no slot sub-components,\nand no `@angular/aria` backing. Double-check the import path whenever `ListItem`/`ListItemComponent` is\nambiguous — for new work, the galactik version documented here is the target."],[2155,"`@sinequa/ui` (the library Galactik replaces) also exports a class named `ButtonComponent` on the same global\n`button` selector — importing both in one file requires aliasing one of them\n(`import { ButtonComponent as UiButton } from \"@sinequa/ui\"`). The two are not interchangeable: `@sinequa/ui`'s\nvariant vocabulary is much larger and shares no compatible values with Galactik's `Button`. Always check the\nimport path."],[2220,"`@sinequa/ui` also exports a `CheckboxComponent` with the identical selector (`checkbox, Checkbox`) and a\n`checked` model of the same name, but it renders a `role=\"checkbox\"` `<div>`-like host with a manual click\nhandler and no real native `<input>` underneath, plus a `size` input galactik's `Checkbox` doesn't have.\nDouble-check the import path (`@sinequa/ui` vs. `@sinequa/galactik`) before assuming which accessibility model\napplies."],[2262,"`@sinequa/ui` also exports a `RadioComponent` with the identical selector (`radio, Radio`) and a `checked` model\nof the same name, but it renders `role=\"radio\"` directly on the host — no real native `<input>`, no `name`\nattribute, no native keyboard roving — and adds a `size` input galactik's `Radio` doesn't have. Double-check the\nimport path before assuming which accessibility model applies."],[2352,"`@sinequa/ui` also exports a `SwitchComponent` with the identical selector (`switch, Switch&#x60;), but its two-way\nbindable state is named &#x2A;*`toggled`**, not `checked`, and it adds a `variant` input galactik's `Switch` doesn't\nhave. The property name difference means example code does not silently port between the two — double-check\nboth the import path and the binding name before reusing it."],[2248,"`@sinequa/ui` also exports an `InputComponent`, but it styles the native `<input>` directly via a `variant`\ninput applied to the element itself — a different composition from galactik's wrap-plus-projected-directives\nmodel. Always check the import path (`@sinequa/ui` vs `@sinequa/galactik`) before assuming which one is in\nscope."],[2834,"`@sinequa/ui` ships its own `PopoverDirective`/`DropdownDirective` with a similar API shape (the same\n`placement`/`offset`/`config`, the same `exportAs` pattern) but a distinct implementation and CSS — unlike\n`Menu`, which has fully migrated, **both implementations genuinely coexist** on this branch. Always check the\nactual import path before assuming which one a `Popover`/`Dropdown` symbol in a file refers to."],[2890,"`@sinequa/ui` ships its own `Sheet` (a Radix-style tree of `sheet-root`/`sheet-content`/… driven by a\n`SheetService`, no native `<dialog>` underneath) exporting the very same class names —\n`SheetComponent`/`SheetHeaderComponent`/`SheetFooterComponent` — and, critically, its root selector is the bare\n`sheet`, which also matches Galactik's `Sheet, sheet` selector pattern. That collision is why this `Sheet` has\n**no lowercase `sheet` alias yet**: importing both into the same template makes two components fight over one\nelement. Always check the import path, and never import a `Sheet*`/`[sheetClose]`/`[sheetTrigger]` symbol from\n`@sinequa/ui` in a file that also uses this one."],[3037,"`@sinequa/ui` ships its own `Tabs`/`TabList`/`Tab`/`TabPanel`, also a thin layer over\n`@angular/aria/tabs`, with an almost identical input surface — but different tokens, and (unlike this one) a\ndefault `flex flex-col overflow-hidden` layout. Always verify the import path before assuming which\nimplementation is in scope."],[2739,"`@sinequa/ui`'s `Dialog` (the library `@sinequa/galactik` is replacing) is a **different implementation** with\nan overlapping vocabulary — the same `createCallable`/`injectCallRef`/`DialogService` concept, even the same\n`DialogEvent`-shaped close events — but a distinct component tree (no separate `DialogBody`/`DialogDescription`\nsplit) and a separate module. Check the import path before assuming two dialog-related symbols are the same\none; do not mix imports from `@sinequa/ui` and `@sinequa/galactik` in the same file."],[1717,"`/`-separated string"],[350,"`/logout` renders the **signed-out** view, not the sign-in form — this matters for the external modes, where\n`SignInComponent` auto-initiates its handshake on mount: rendering it on `/logout` would immediately\nre-authenticate the user, a spinner that loops back in. See Signed Out."],[2851,"`#panel=\"sheet\"` reads `SheetComponent` off its `exportAs`, then `open()` (an alias for `showModal()`) shows it.\nThere is no intermediate content element — `SheetHeader`/`SheetBody` project straight into `<Sheet>`."],[144,"`↑` / `↓` move from one row to the next."],[746,"`<aggregation-date>` is what `<Aggregation>` delegates to for a date column — see\nAggregation. Unlike the list and tree variants, its options are a **radio\nlist** (never a multi-select), and it opens a dialog for a custom range instead of paging through buckets."],[839,"`<Aggregation>` (and `<aggregation-list>`/`<aggregation-tree>` directly) hides itself **unconditionally** when\nthe aggregation is **missing** — no entry in the store matching `name`/`column`, most often a typo, or an\nempty `[aggregation]` in controlled mode. This is different from resolving to zero items, which stays visible\nby default. `hideWhenEmpty` has no effect on a missing aggregation; check the `name`/`column` values against\nthe app's aggregation configuration first."],[820,"`<Aggregation>` does not render anything itself — it resolves which of three sub-components actually renders,\nthen delegates:"],[623,"`<AggregationChart>` does three things only: `toSeries()` turns the aggregation into two parallel arrays\n(pure, testable, no dependency of its own), a `computed()` builds a Chart.js `ChartConfiguration` from them, and\n`<ChartCanvas>` owns the actual canvas lifecycle. It never fetches and\nnever touches the DOM directly."],[863,"`<applied-filters>` shows what the query **is** filtered by, one chip per value. Contrast with\nFilters bar, which shows what a user **can** filter on."],[892,"`<aside-filters>` stacks several Aggregation facets as a native `<details>`/`<summary>`\naccordion — the sidebar counterpart of Filters bar, for a layout where facets sit in a\npersistent column or drawer rather than a row of buttons."],[1469,"`<backdrop>` renders nothing but a dimmed, full-screen overlay, and shows or hides itself in response to\n`BackdropService.show()`/`hide()`. It is meant to sit once near the root of the application, behind whatever\noverlay (a custom stacked panel, say) needs a scrim."],[76,"`<bookmark-button>` is a custom element; only the native `<button>` inside it is focusable, and a caller that\nmanages focus itself (an `@angular/aria` grid, a manual roving tabindex) can move focus onto it but not remove\nit from the natural tab order on top of that. Set `[tabIndex]=\"-1\"` (or whatever the roving pattern expects)\nexplicitly rather than assuming the host element's own absence from the DOM tabindex chain is enough."],[617,"`<ChartCanvas>` — the shared lifecycle"],[472,"`<collections-manager>` is the page-sized way to manage collections: every collection as a grid or a list, a\nfilter, creation and deletion, and a detail view that lists the documents of one collection: the user can rename the\ncollection, preview a document beside the list, and take documents out. From the list or from the detail, a collection can be **applied to the filters**, which opens the search\npage scoped to it. For a compact list in a popover, use the collections list instead."],[1790,"`<converter-select>` renders only when `previewMultiConversion` is on **and** more than one configured\nconverter matches the currently loaded preview's available conversions — otherwise the host hides itself\nentirely, so it can be dropped into a toolbar without leaving a gap when there is nothing to choose between."],[1833,"`<document-locator>` renders a document's location within a tree-shaped aggregation (a folder structure, a\ncollection hierarchy) as breadcrumbs. Clicking a segment updates the query's filters and navigates to that\nlocation, and segments that don't fit the available width move into an overflow dropdown automatically."],[2061,"`<document-preview-panel>` previews one document. `<workspace-detail>` opens it beside the list when a document is\nchosen; use the panel on its own to preview a `WorkspaceDocument` anywhere else."],[1478,"`<ErrorComponent>` is a route target — mount it on an `\"error\"` path and navigate to it (optionally with a\n`message` query param) whenever the application cannot continue."],[581,"`<feedback>` (`SearchFeedbackComponent`) is a compact menu trigger. Its entries send a thumbs-up or thumbs-down\nfor the results on screen, or open a dialog where the user types a free-form message. Everything goes through\nthe audit, nothing is stored client-side."],[914,"`<filter-button>` is what Filters bar renders one of, per authorized aggregation: a pill\nbutton that opens the aggregation's facet in a native popover."],[936,"`<filters-bar>` renders one filter button per authorized aggregation, in a group of buttons you walk through with `Tab`, and folds whatever does not fit into a \"More\" button. It shows what a user\n**can** filter on — contrast with Applied filters, which shows what the query **is**\ncurrently filtered by."],[957,"`<filters-panel>` is the content of the filters column of a search page: a header with a close button, a switch between two\nviews, and the view itself. It holds the Aside filters and the Quick filters of the\nuser, which it drives from the same stores as they do: nothing to wire but the count of documents and what to do on close."],[638,"`<Heatmap>` is the one to reach for first — it costs no dependency and is fully accessible.\n`<AggregationHeatmap>` exists for the case `<Heatmap>` cannot serve: a matrix large enough that hundreds of DOM\nnodes become the bottleneck. Canvas rendering is flat in the cell count; a DOM grid is not."],[677,"`<Heatmap>` never fetches and never transforms the aggregation itself — `buildHeatmap()`, a pure function, does\nboth jobs: it parses `encoding`, ranks and caps both axes at `maxX`/`maxY`, and returns an indexed grid the\ntemplate renders directly."],[88,"`<history-panel>` is the user's search history as a panel beside the application's sidebar: the recent searches\ngrouped by day, a **All / Favorites** switch, a star to mark a search as favorite and a button to delete one. It\nreads and writes `UserSettingsStore` itself and re-runs a search through the router, so the only thing to wire is\nwhether it is open."],[1971,"`<lexiq-workspaces>` dispatches `lexiq-workspaces:workspace-created` and `lexiq-workspaces:workspace-updated` (detail:\n`{ workspace }`), and `lexiq-workspaces:ask-followup`. All the events bubble and cross a shadow boundary, which\n`@angular/elements` does not do by itself."],[1305,"`<loading-dots>` is the loader of the preview while a document is fetched, converted and scrolled to its page, of the\nExtracts tab of the find results, and of the **Load more** button of the list and tree facets: three dots that wave one\nafter the other, in place of a spinner."],[274,"`<manage-access>` edits two lists of principal ids, `owners` and `readers`, that your component owns. It shows\nthe people and groups who have access, a search to add more, a role menu and a remove button per row, and the\nswitches that make the item public."],[996,"`<more-button>` picks its container by context, never the same one twice on a given viewport:"],[994,"`<more-button>` renders the \"More\" trigger Filters bar shows once more filters are\nauthorized than fit — its content, `<more>`, is a list of the filters that opens one at a time (or, with\n`layout=\"accordion\"`, the same stacked-facet accordion as Aside filters), shown inside an overlay\ninstead of a persistent sidebar."],[989,"`<More>` alone, inside a custom container"],[116,"`<multi-selection-toolbar>` is the bar that slides up from the bottom of the screen once the user has selected\nmore than one document — add them all to a collection, export them, or (when the app's assistant feature\nallows it) attach them to an AI overview. It reads the current selection from `SelectionStore` and needs\nnothing wired to know when to appear."],[1507,"`<navbar-tabs>` renders one tab per child route of a given router `path` — there is no tab list to pass in,\nonly a `data` object per route. The active tab tracks the current URL, and whatever doesn't fit collapses into\na \"⋮\" menu instead of clipping."],[1899,"`<NoResult>` is a purely presentational, input-less message shown when a search genuinely matched nothing —\nsee Query for telling that state apart from a failed request, which is the caller's\nresponsibility, not this component's."],[1330,"`<overflow-actions>` is the row of secondary actions next to the title of a document, in a preview header or a result\ncard: the bookmark toggle, a button that copies the link of the document and a button that expands the view. They are\naligned 24px icon buttons; once there are more than `maxInline` of them, the extra ones go into a \"…\" menu that closes\nthe row."],[1359,"`<preview-actions>` is the bar `<preview-content>` shows above its document: the \"Find text…\" field with its\nresults panel on the left; on the right the converter select, a highlights popover, the host's own actions, the\nzoom buttons and the page navigation. It is everything a zoomable, searchable `PreviewService` needs, all in\nview — when the room runs out, the right-hand group wraps under the field and stays right-aligned."],[1791,"`<preview-content>` already embeds this dropdown internally (through `<preview-actions>`) and owns the\nresulting conversion itself — reach for `<converter-select>` directly only when building a **custom** preview\nsurface that does not use `<preview-content>`."],[1385,"`<preview-content>` is the whole document preview surface: it loads the previewed document, picks the right\nrendering path (a converted-document iframe, or a Markdown fallback when no conversion is available), and\nshows the action bar (`<preview-actions>`, which holds the page navigator) above it, on a grey desk the\ndocument sits on like a white sheet."],[1393,"`<preview-find-panel>` is an outlet: it renders the card that `<preview-content>` offers through\n`PreviewService.findPanelTemplate`, and nothing (no room, no gap) while there is none. It can sit anywhere in the\nhost's template — next to the header as well as next to the document — so the card can span the full height of the\npreview, header included:"],[1409,"`<preview-header>` is the header of a document preview: the title (a link when you give it an `href`), the\ndocument's actions next to it, a few always-visible lines of metadata, and the rest behind a **More details**\ntoggle. It owns only the layout — which metadata fields to show, and what renders them, is entirely up to the\nconsuming app."],[1016,"`<quick-filters>` is a pill that opens the user's **quick filters**: named sets of filters (say \"Recent PDFs\" = PDF plus\nthe last 30 days) that one click applies again. It is made to lead a `<filters-bar appearance=\"pill\">`, in its\n`filtersBarStart` slot, and wears the same outline as the filters beside it."],[141,"`<recent-searches>` lists the searches a user has actually run, most recent first — populated automatically by\n`UserSettingsStore` as the user searches, with no explicit \"save\" step. It shares its row shape (filter-count\nbadge, relative date) and its reopen mechanism with saved searches."],[184,"`<search-box>` is the search field of an application: a pill-shaped bar with a popup of two tabs, **Suggestions**\n(the user's own searches and the suggest queries, grouped by category) and **Saved** (the saved searches). It reads\nthe app configuration and the user settings itself and, once a search is validated, navigates to the results page with\nthe text and the filters, so there is nothing to wire for the common case."],[1936,"`<sort-selector>` reads which sort choices apply from the **result** it is given — a tab search and a regular\nquery can expose different lists, and the component resolves the right one instead of the caller having to\nknow which applies."],[1952,"`<sponsored-results>` fetches and renders the application's configured sponsored links for the current query —\na small, self-contained list with no filtering or paging of its own."],[2667,"`<SwipeAction>` wraps a row and lets a finger drag it to the left or to the right: a coloured layer with the action\nappears under it, and releasing the row past a threshold triggers the action (the iOS Mail gesture). It is what stands in\nfor a hover-only action on a touch screen, where nothing hovers."],[2564,"`<tag-remove>`:"],[1535,"`<theme-selector>` has no default trigger content — its template is `<button [menuTrigger]=\"…\"><ng-content /></button>`.\nProject a label, an icon, or both between the tags; an empty `<theme-selector scope=\"main\" />` is a button with\nnothing to click on."],[1533,"`<theme-selector>` projects whatever you put inside it as the menu's trigger button — there is no default\nlabel or icon — and opens a menu of every theme registered for the given `scope`, each row swatched with that\ntheme's `primary` color."],[1543,"`<theme-toggle>` is a hidden checkbox bound through Signal Forms, with an icon and a \"Dark mode\" label doing the\nvisible work — checking it calls `ThemeStore.setDarkMode()` for the given scope."],[1416,"`<tr slot=\"summary\">`"],[2117,"`<upload-tracker>` has to be in the page: the service only fills the store. `<workspaces-shell>` includes it;\nwith the parts, add `<upload-tracker />` once, anywhere that stays on screen."],[2006,"`<workspace-list>` has two outputs: `opened` (the `Workspace` chosen) and `createRequested`. `<workspace-detail>` has\nfour: `back`, `deleted` (the `Workspace`), `workspaceUpdated` (the `Workspace` as the server now has it) and\n`askFollowUp` (`AskFollowUp`)."],[2000,"`<workspace-list>` shows every workspace the user can reach, as a grid or a list; `<workspace-detail>` shows one\nworkspace and its documents. Use them instead of `WorkspacesShell` when the two belong\nin different places of your layout, or when navigation is the router's business."],[2038,"`<workspace-mention-search>` takes `size`, `textSize`, `placeholder` and `aria-label`. `<workspace-mention-new>` takes the button's\nown `variant`, `scheme` and `size`, and content placed inside replaces its icon and label."],[2047,"`<workspace-mention-search>`, `-select-all`, `-new` and `-tabs` read the state of the nearest `<workspace-mention>` above them; there is none."],[2036,"`<workspace-mention-tabs>` draws the categories as tabs. To draw them another way, read `tabs`, `tab` and `counts()` from the\nstate: `all`, owned (`creator` or `owner`) and shared (`reader`)."],[2042,"`<workspace-mention>` has two outputs: `selectionChanged` (`MentionSelection`: `ids` and `count`) and `workspaceCreated` (the `Workspace`)."],[2025,"`<workspace-mention>` is the workspaces as rows to tick. Empty, it is a ready-made menu; with your own content inside, it only\nprovides the state — search, category, ticks, \"select all\", \"new workspace\" — and you lay out the pieces and the rows as you like."],[2027,"`<workspace-mention>` owns the state, and everything inside it reads it. The state comes from `injectWorkspaceMention()`, so a\ncomponent that wants no markup of ours can call that directly."],[2130,"`<workspaces-shell>` is the piece that decides where things go: choosing a workspace opens it, \"Add workspace\"\nopens the dialog and then the new workspace, leaving a workspace goes back to the list."],[1621,"`⌥`+`e`/`u`/`i`/`n` (and others) are dead keys on macOS, used to type `é`, `ü`, `ñ`. Registering `Alt+e` steals\nthat combination system-wide for every app using this service. Prefer `Alt+`*digit* instead, which is free in\nevery browser and compared by physical key."],[2873,"`100dvh`"],[2870,"`100dvh` minus a gutter"],[2872,"`100dvw`"],[2869,"`100dvw` minus a gutter"],[213,"`12`"],[1040,"`8`"],[538,"`add()`/`remove()` call the labels web service directly and set an error flag on any rejection — most often\nthe current user lacking the rights the server enforces for that visibility (public labels usually require an\nelevated right). Check `LabelService.canHandleLabels()` before assuming the field itself is broken."],[2116,"`addFiles` returns the tracked id of each file, in order. `cancel(id)`, `retry(id)` and `resumeStalled()` act on\ntracked uploads. `watch(workspaceId)` tells the service which workspace is open, so that the status polling is scoped\nto it and starts when that workspace already holds documents that are not finished — a page reload loses every\nlocal upload handle, and the listing is then the only way to know. `<workspace-detail>` calls it for the workspace it shows."],[2373,"`addOnBlur` covers a click on a Save button, which takes the focus first. A form that submits with the field still\nfocused — Enter in *another* field, or a programmatic `submit()` — has to call `commit()` first:\n`viewChild(TagInputComponent).commit()`."],[678,"`AggregationLike` is a **structural** type, not an import of `@sinequa/atomic`'s `Aggregation` — a real\n`Aggregation` is assignable to it with no cast, so nothing here depends on that package. The same pattern\nrecurs across every component in this section: a pure, dependency-free data module (`buildHeatmap`,\n`toSeries`, `buildTimeline`, `buildTagCloud`) does the parsing, and the component only renders."],[554,"`AlertForm` is a callable, not a component — there is nothing to place in a template and no `opened` flag to\ndrive. The returned promise resolves with the `DialogEvent` that closed it: `\"dialog-confirm\"` when the alert\nwas saved, `\"dialog-cancel\"` otherwise. The dialog only hosts the form: the fields, the validation and the save\nare those of `AlertFormComponent`, which you can place anywhere (see Using the form outside a dialog)."],[560,"`AlertsComponent` reads its list from `UserSettingsStore`. Its `dialog` input (default `true`) decides what a click on a row or on \"Create\" does: open the `AlertForm` dialog, or — with `[dialog]=\"false\"` — open nothing and emit `editRequested` (the alert's index) or `createRequested`, so that you show `<alert-form>` wherever you like. The Settings dialog does that in its Alerts panel. `AlertDialog` receives its index\nthrough the callable's props and reports only through the resolved promise. `AlertFormComponent` (`<alert-form>`):"],[1758,"`allowChangePassword` alone is not sufficient — the computed also requires a `credentials` auth mode and an\neditable partition. Check all three before assuming the flag is inert (unlike the four flags that genuinely\nare — see Feature flags)."],[1581,"`APP_FEATURES` is deprecated — it is a separate, older injection token, unrelated to `general.features` and not\nread by this dialog or by `AppStore`. Use `general.features` (edited here, read via `AppStore.general()`)\ninstead."],[1030,"`appearance`"],[942,"`appearance=\"pill\"` makes every filter button (and the \"More\" button) a rounded outlined pill that stays that pill, with no fill, once its\nfilter holds a selection (the `+N` badge it then carries would not sit on every coloured background). `scheme` picks its colour family — one of the twelve schemes of the galactik `Tag`, `sage` by\ndefault — and it moves the border and the hover (the tint of an open popover included) together; the text is\nunchanged. A filter that holds a selection stays the same outlined pill, with no fill. Anything marked `filtersBarStart` is projected **before** the group of buttons, not inside it: it\nis a tab stop of its own, and it\nstays visible when the bar has no filter to show. `hasContent()` tells a page whether the bar renders anything."],[1231,"`ApplicationService` (injected internally by `bootstrapApp` when `createRoutes: true`)\nreads this token to know which component renders each tab of the query's `tabSearch` configuration, and which\none is the root layout (`isRoot: true`) wrapping the whole `search` route."],[1045,"`applied`"],[1222,"`AppStore` must already be initialized (`AppStore.initialize()`, which `bootstrapApp`/`ApplicationService`\ncall) before this resolver runs, or `getDefaultQuery()` has nothing to read yet. A route protected by\n`AuthGuard` and reached only after bootstrap completes does not hit this; a resolver\nregistered on a route that can be reached before bootstrap finishes can."],[2348,"`aria-label`: a text, for a switch with no visible label."],[2349,"`aria-labelledby`: the id of a visible element elsewhere, for a label that must not toggle the switch\n(for example the header of an expandable card). It takes precedence over `aria-label` and over the projected content."],[1060,"`ATOMIC_CLIENT` defaults to the client backing `@sinequa/atomic`'s free functions (`login()`, `fetchQuery()`,\n…), whose configuration **is** `globalConfig`. An application that never calls `provideAtomicClient()` behaves\nexactly as before — there is nothing to migrate."],[1111,"`ATOMIC_SCOPED_PROVIDERS` is exported, for an application that wants to add its own scoped service alongside\nthe library's."],[1103,"`ATOMIC_SCOPED_PROVIDERS` re-creates 21 providers alongside the client: seven stores, `UserSettingsPersistence` (the\nhelper that tracks whether this scope's settings have been read, which gates writing them back) and thirteen\nservices. Every one of them is `providedIn: \"root\"`, so a store left at the root would keep the **root** client and\nsilently read and write the *first* backend from inside the second backend's subtree."],[2804,"`atomic-ui`'s legacy `Menu`/`MenuContent`/`MenuItem` docs describe an older implementation, but on this branch\n`Menu` has already been fully migrated to Galactik across `@sinequa/atomic-angular` — there is no active\nduplicate to reconcile here, unlike `Sheet` and `Popover`. Any remaining `atomic-ui` `Menu` reference you find\nis historical."],[1183,"`auditInterceptorFn` — audit metadata"],[1226,"`AuditService` — sending audit events by hand"],[29,"`AuthGuard()` checks the session, loads the signed-in user, and — in `credentials` mode — redirects to\nchange-password when the session's password has expired; it never guards `/login`/`/logout` themselves. See\nSign In for embedding the form outside a full page, and Authentication\nflows for how the guard, the interceptor, sign-in and logout fit together\nend to end."],[304,"`AuthGuard()` returns a `CanActivateFn` that protects a route: it checks the user is authenticated, ensures\n`PrincipalStore` is loaded, and — in `credentials` mode — redirects to the change-password view when the\nsession's password has expired."],[322,"`authInterceptorFn` serves the **Angular `HttpClient` channel**, which `@sinequa/atomic`'s own client does not\nsee. The library's stores and services no longer use `HttpClient` — they go through the client, which sets\nthese headers itself, per client — but `@sinequa/agent` and `@sinequa/assistant` build their requests with\n`HttpClient`, as may your own code. Without this interceptor, those requests leave with no CSRF token and the\nserver answers 401."],[341,"`AuthPageComponent` switches between the sign-in, change-password, forgot-password and signed-out views (from\nthe route path and the `mode` query param) — in particular `/logout` renders the signed-out confirmation, not\nthe sign-in form. `SignInComponent` alone only renders the credentials form and ignores the route."],[2190,"`Autocomplete` fills the gap `SelectMenu` deliberately doesn't cover: `SelectMenu` is a\nfixed/synchronous options list behind a non-editable trigger, while `Autocomplete` is always multi-selection\nand always backed by an async `loader`. Visually the two are the same component family — both wear the same\n`selectWrapVariants` wrap, so label, field, popup, rows and hint come from one design-system source of truth.\nIt is the one component behind every \"type, pick from a list\" field of the library, and it replaces `SearchSelect`."],[2191,"`Autocomplete` never mutates its own applied values. `selected` is input-only, and `picked`/`removed` only\nreport the user's intent — the host calls its own backend (with error handling) before reflecting the change\nback into `selected`. This mirrors a real \"add labels to a document\" flow."],[443,"`autoStart` defaults to `true` — in `sso`/`oauth`/`saml`/`bearer` mode the component starts the handshake as\r\nsoon as it mounts, which for a full-page redirect means navigating away from the dialog entirely. Set\r\n`[autoStart]=\"false\"` whenever the component is embedded rather than owning the route."],[2407,"`Avatar` has no built-in slot for a status dot; it composes with `Badge` through a relatively-positioned\nwrapper — the same pattern the design system uses everywhere a status pill is glued to another element."],[2404,"`Avatar` represents a user or entity as a picture or initials, switching between the two automatically as the\nimage loads, fails, or is absent — no manual state handling required. It is composed of the `Avatar`\ncontainer plus two projected sub-components, `AvatarImage` and `AvatarFallback`."],[2410,"`Avatar`'s template projects content through two typed `<ng-content select>` slots, tied to the exact tag\nspelling `AvatarImage`/`AvatarFallback` — any other projected content (plain text, a different element) is\nsilently dropped rather than falling through to a default slot. The kebab-case (`<avatar-image>`) and\nlowercase (`<avatarimage>`) selector aliases exist for standalone use elsewhere, but are not guaranteed to\nland in `Avatar`'s projection slots — stick to the PascalCase forms when nesting inside `<avatar>`."],[2406,"`AvatarFallback` also inherits `size`/`fill`/`scheme` from its parent `<avatar>` through the `AVATAR_REF`\ntoken, so a size set once on `<avatar>` sizes the fallback too — set a prop directly on `<AvatarFallback>` to\noverride just that one."],[2409,"`AvatarImage` additionally takes `src` (required), `alt`, `width`, `height`. `AvatarFallback` accepts explicit\n`size`/`fill`/`scheme` overrides, inherited from the parent `<avatar>` when omitted."],[2405,"`AvatarImage` preloads its `src` through an off-DOM `Image()` probe before anything renders, so there is never\na layout-shifting broken-image icon. `AvatarFallback` is shown until that probe resolves, and stays shown\npermanently if it fails — or if there is no `AvatarImage` at all, for an initials-only avatar."],[2411,"`AvatarImageComponent`'s probe calls `console.log(\"Image failed to load\", src)` — not `console.error` — on\n`onerror`. Expect console noise in views with unreliable avatar URLs; there is no output to intercept this\ninstead."],[1471,"`backdropVisible` is not an `@Input()` — it is a `@HostBinding` the component sets itself, subscribed to\n`BackdropService.isVisible`. The only way to show or hide the backdrop is through the service's `show()`/`hide()`,\nnever a template binding on the component."],[2421,"`Badge` is a small pastille apposed on another element — a numeric counter (notifications, result counts) or a\nstatus icon (online, verified, featured). It is a bare directive with no template: it only contributes host\nclasses (and, optionally, ARIA attributes) to whichever tag you write it on."],[1181,"`bodyInterceptorFn` — the interface language"],[204,"`boolean`"],[216,"`boolean`"],[220,"`boolean`"],[252,"`boolean`"],[1131,"`bootstrapApp()` is the one call most applications register as an `provideAppInitializer`: it resolves the\nauthentication mode, signs the user in, and initializes the application's stores in the right order, so no\ncomponent has to guard against a half-initialized application."],[27,"`bootstrapApp()` resolves the authentication mode, signs the user in if a session already exists, and\ninitializes the application's stores before the first view renders — see Provide a\nclient and Bootstrap the application\nfor the full contract, including what to do when part of the configuration is only known at runtime."],[1134,"`bootstrapApp` keeps the client's `uiLanguage` on the interface language rather than a value fixed at\nstart-up: it follows `TranslocoService.langChanges$` for as long as the application runs, which is also what\nthe request body interceptor and the `syslang`/`translocoDate` pipes read on their own side (see\nCross-cutting pipes)."],[2578,"`BreakpointObserverService` answers one question as a signal: is the viewport narrower than the `md`\nbreakpoint (768px)? It exists for the cases CSS cannot express — choosing a component (a sheet instead of a\nside panel), a placement (`bottom-start` instead of `left-start`), a size input."],[1272,"`buildQuery(partial)` resolves the query's `name` by checking, in order: `partial.name`,\n`getQueryNameFromRoute()` (the active route's `queryName` route data, or the current tab's `t` query\nparameter matched against the app's `search` route children), then the URL's own stored parameters — and\nmerges everything else from the URL underneath your `partial`. Both must run inside an injection context."],[1266,"`buildQuery` / `getQueryNameFromRoute` — a query from the current route"],[707,"`buildTagCloud()` merges every supplied aggregation's items into one ranked list, keeping track of which\naggregation each entry came from (`entry.source`) so the cloud can color entity types consistently. Font size\nis a direct, linear function of `entry.weight` — no measurement pass, which is what makes this component cheap\nenough to not need its own bundle split."],[2435,"`Bullet` is a colored dot: a status, a category or a priority, shown next to a label. It is a bare directive\nwith no template — you write it as its own empty element. Think of it as the dot half of\n`Badge`: the same twelve schemes, the same two fills, without the content — a bullet and a\nbadge of the same scheme share the same background tokens."],[2149,"`Button` is a directive on the native `button` selector, not a wrapped component: importing it into a\ncomponent's `imports` restyles every `<button>` that component's own template renders directly, while leaving\n`type`, `disabled`, form association and keyboard behavior exactly as the platform provides them."],[282,"`canMakePublic` (default `true`) shows the switch; `publicValue` changes the reader value it controls. While the\nitem is public, or a custom switch is on, a notice says that owners will still be the only ones who can modify it."],[369,"`ChangePasswordComponent` (`<change-password>`) takes the same inputs except `showActions`. It always draws its card."],[367,"`ChangePasswordFormComponent` (`<change-password-form>`):"],[25,"`chart.js`/`chartjs-chart-matrix` and `markdown-it` are also peer dependencies, but only needed if you use the\nChart.js-based data visualization components or the preview's Markdown fallback —\nskip them otherwise. Likewise `vanilla-calendar-pro`, with the `@sinequa/atomic-angular/styles/calendar.css`\nstylesheet, is only needed for the custom range of a date facet."],[640,"`chartjs-chart-matrix` ships no other chart in this library needs, so it lives in its own tertiary entry point\n(`@sinequa/atomic-angular/charts/heatmap`) rather than the shared `/charts` one — an application drawing only\nbar charts never resolves it."],[2219,"`Checkbox` stops click propagation on its host, so nesting it inside a clickable row (a list item, say) never\nalso fires the row's handler. `Radio` and `Switch` do **not** do this — the same nesting on those two\ncomponents will trigger the row's click handler as well."],[2216,"`Checkbox` wraps a native `<input type=\"checkbox\">` inside an accessible `<label>`, with built-in support for a\nsecondary hint line and an indeterminate (\"mixed\") visual state."],[1065,"`client.auth.isRedirectPending()` tells a provider handshake apart from a plain missing session.\n`client.storagePrefix` reads back the prefix namespacing this client's storage keys."],[2882,"`closed` emits a `DialogEvent` once per close, however triggered. `closeEnd` emits once the exit animation has\nsettled and the content has left the DOM — use it, not `closed`, to release anything rendered inside the\npanel."],[2717,"`closest(\"[popover]\")` keeps the component reusable: inside a popover it mounts there, anywhere else it falls\nback to `document.body`. `ConfirmDialog` is the one from the minimal example — nothing in\nthe dialog itself changes."],[511,"`CollectionsComponent` has no outputs — clicking a collection navigates directly via the router."],[507,"`CollectionsComponent` itself never adds documents to a collection — that is a separate entry point, callable\nfrom anywhere a set of articles is available (a result row, a multi-selection toolbar):"],[1812,"`correction`"],[995,"`count` is how many authorized filters are already shown elsewhere (typically the bar's own `filtersCount`) —\n`<more-button>` renders everything **beyond** that."],[1006,"`count` must match what the bar itself actually shows — typically its own `filtersCount` (or the app-wide\n`FILTERS_BREAKPOINT` it defaults to). `<more-button>` has no way to know what another component rendered; it\nonly knows how many to skip."],[2712,"`createCallable<P, R>` types both the arguments passed into `call(props)` and the value the promise resolves\nwith — here a `{ name: string } | null` result, `null` on cancel."],[660,"`dashSecondary` renders every second series dashed, the \"previous period\" convention, and pairs it with the\nsame color as the series it compares against rather than doubling the palette."],[1653,"`data` picks, among eight standard fields, the ones the form shows. These are the only values it accepts:"],[245,"`debounced`"],[1279,"`debouncedSignal` is `@deprecated` — Angular's own `debounced()` (`@angular/core`, experimental since v22)\ncovers the same need natively. It returns a `Resource<T>`, not a plain `Signal<T>`: read the debounced value\nthrough `.value()` rather than calling it directly."],[146,"`Delete` removes it, as the cross does — and `Backspace` too on a Mac, whose keyboard has no dedicated\n`Delete` key."],[2497,"`depth` indents a row by 20px per level, and `ListItemExpander` renders the rotating expand/collapse chevron.\nIn today's listbox-backed `List`, tree behavior is **style only** — expansion state and which children render\nis entirely up to the consumer; there is no `role=\"tree\"` keyboard semantics yet (a future\n`@angular/aria/tree`-backed variant will add it)."],[2725,"`DialogComponent`'s template wraps all projected content in `@if (isOpen())` — closing it fully **destroys**\nthe projected tree rather than hiding it. Any local component state inside a declaratively-wired (non-callable)\ndialog body resets on every reopen."],[2704,"`DialogContent` never needs a `for` or an explicit id to associate its title: `DialogTitle` publishes its own\nid (generated, or set explicitly) into the `MODAL_LABEL` token it shares with `DialogContent`, which reads it\nback for `aria-labelledby`. The same token pattern lets `DialogHeader`'s built-in close button — and any\ncustom sub-component you project — close the dialog by injecting `DIALOG_REF`, without ever depending on\n`DialogComponent` itself (see Pitfalls)."],[2731,"`DialogContent`, like `DialogBody`, `DialogFooter`, `DialogTitle` and `DialogDescription`, is a bare\n`@Directive`, not a `@Component` — it has no template of its own, so its children are the literal markup you\nwrote between the tags, not content projected through an `<ng-content>`. Only `DialogHeader` is a real\n`@Component`, with two `<ng-content select>` slots plus the built-in close button."],[2732,"`DialogService.syncStack()` tags every instance except the bottom-most with an `atomic-dialog--behind` host\nclass, meant to leave exactly one visible backdrop for a whole stack — but `@sinequa/galactik` ships no CSS for\nit. Nothing acts on that class unless your application defines the rule."],[2983,"`disabled` is a purely visual/semantic flag — native anchors have no real `disabled` attribute, so the link\nstays present in the DOM with its `href` intact unless you remove that separately. `aria-disabled` and\n`tabindex=\"-1\"` only take it out of the tab order and suppress hover/active/click styling."],[3004,"`disabled` reflects `aria-disabled=\"true\"` and sets `tabindex=\"-1\"` — the same contract as\n`Link`'s own `disabled`."],[2502,"`disabled`, `readonly`, `softDisabled`, `wrap`, `orientation`, `typeaheadDelay` are forwarded from\n`@angular/aria/listbox`'s `Listbox` and default to whatever that package ships. `ListItem` additionally takes\n`depth` (tree indentation, 20px/level) and forwards `value` (required), `disabled`, `label` from `Option`."],[2951,"`document` scroll listener never removed"],[536,"`EditLabels` reads the labels web service configuration itself and renders a public field, a private field, or\nboth, depending on what the app actually exposes — the caller does not need to know the field names."],[2001,"`EditWorkspace.call()` without an injector needs `provideCallable()` from `@sinequa/galactik` in the application's\nproviders; the components themselves pass their own injector and never do."],[2532,"`endApproached` fires once keyboard focus reaches the last `rowsBeforeEnd` articles, so the list keeps growing\nfor a keyboard user who never touches the mouse wheel."],[145,"`Enter` (or `Space`) opens the row, as a click does."],[1746,"`Enter` saves the row being edited, and `Escape` cancels it. The form usually lives in a dialog, which closes on\n`Escape`: while a row is being edited the key belongs to the field, so cancelling the edit does **not** close the\ndialog. On a row that is not being edited, `Escape` closes the dialog as it would anywhere else."],[1182,"`errorInterceptorFn` — 401 recovery"],[1191,"`errorInterceptorFn` retries **at most once per request**, tracked through an `HttpContextToken`\n(`AUTH_RETRIED`) carried on the request itself (never sent to the server). A second `401` on the retried\nrequest propagates instead of triggering another `signIn()`. Without this guard, an endpoint that keeps\nrejecting even after a successful re-authentication would re-trigger sign-in on every response, forever."],[121,"`Escape` inside the bar clears the selection. The bar then becomes `inert` and drops the focus, so the\n`cleared` output is where the page puts it back (for example on the results)."],[1612,"`event.key` is the **character** a key produces — layout-dependent, so `\"1\"` unshifted is `&` on an AZERTY\nkeyboard. `event.code` is the **physical position** — layout-independent. Digits are compared by `event.code`\nhere specifically because a written `Alt+1` should reach the same physical key everywhere; every other key is\ncompared by `event.key`, since a printed letter is what a person actually reads off their keyboard."],[251,"`expandedChange`"],[1815,"`expansion`"],[2916,"`exportAs=\"tooltip\"` gives the instance: `show()`, `hide()` and the `isOpen` signal."],[1576,"`FeatureFlagsDialogComponent` reads `AppStore.general()` and lets an **administrator** edit it live: every\nedit rewrites the whole `general` object back onto the store through the same source it was read from (the\n`general` customJSON if the app has one, otherwise inline `data.general`), so unknown keys the dialog does not\nknow about survive the round-trip. A non-administrator still gets the dialog, with an \"admin only\" notice\ninstead of the editor — there is no separate access check to remember on the caller's side."],[1200,"`fileSize` — bytes to a unit and a magnitude"],[1027,"`FilterScheme`"],[941,"`filtersCount` can also be set application-wide through the `FILTERS_BREAKPOINT` injection token, rather than\nrepeating it on every `<filters-bar>`:"],[3006,"`floating`/`inset` collapse differently from the default `sidebar` variant: instead of narrowing to an icon\nrail, the *whole* panel slides off-screen (and is marked `inert` while hidden, so its own header trigger\ncannot still be reached by keyboard). An app using either variant with collapse therefore needs a **third**\ntrigger placed outside the `<Sidebar>`, shown only while `collapsed()` is true."],[390,"`ForgotPasswordComponent` (`<forgot-password>`) has no inputs: it always draws its card."],[388,"`ForgotPasswordFormComponent` (`<forgot-password-form>`):"],[2868,"`full`"],[1658,"`fullName`"],[1569,"`general.features` — boolean flags"],[1570,"`general.feedback` — six independent switches"],[2875,"`getAnimations()` rather than a `transitionend` listener: a transition that is **interrupted** emits\n`transitioncancel`, never `transitionend`, and one that never starts — under `prefers-reduced-motion`, where\nevery duration is zero — emits nothing at all. Both were measured on a consumer, where the projected content\nstayed mounted for good inside a hidden panel because `closeEnd` never fired to release it."],[1267,"`getCurrentPath` / `getCurrentQueryName` — reading route metadata"],[1781,"`getFromUserSettingsForText` groups its results by `recent-searches`, `saved-searches` and `bookmarks` — enough\nto render each group under its own heading, or to merge and cap them, as the caller prefers."],[1461,"`getTextChunks(...)` returns `Observable<TextChunk[]>`."],[1436,"`gotoPage`/`nextPage`/`prevPage`/`firstPage`/`lastPage` all message the iframe directly, and stay between the\nfirst and the last page; `currentPage` and `totalPages` are signals a toolbar can read to render page numbers.\n`currentPage` follows the page being read as the user scrolls through the document, and `isFirstPage` /\n`isLastPage` tell the ends apart, to disable a button."],[1668,"`group`"],[691,"`Heatmap`, `TagCloud` and `WordCloud` need no charting dependency and ship from the main\n`@sinequa/atomic-angular` entry point. `AggregationChart`, `AggregationTimeline` and `AggregationHeatmap` are\nbuilt on Chart.js, an **optional peer dependency** — they ship from a separate\n`@sinequa/atomic-angular/charts` entry point (and, for the matrix-based heatmap, a further\n`@sinequa/atomic-angular/charts/heatmap`), so an application that never imports from there never needs\n`chart.js` installed."],[288,"`hidden`"],[2938,"`hideDelay` (the old name still works)"],[1589,"`HIGHLIGHTS` configures the colors the document preview uses to highlight five categories of text — entities