ticle property the picked/removed labels are read from and written back to — it has\nto match the field the labels web service configuration actually uses for that visibility (public or private),\nwhich is exactly what `EditLabels` resolves for you (see Recipes)."],[2623,"`layout` fires on every pointer move during a drag, not only when it settles. Persist through `autoSaveId`\ninstead of a manual handler, or debounce anything expensive you attach to it directly."],[1004,"`layout=\"accordion\"` keeps every filter as a collapsible section of one scrolling list. Its facet headers are dressed like\nthe rows of the list: same height, weight and hover tint, and a focus ring around the whole row (the chevron is the tab\nstop), so the two layouts read alike."],[2854,"`left` / `right` (width)"],[2862,"`lg`"],[2942,"`life`, default 0 — **a tooltip no longer closes by itself** (the old name still works)"],[2979,"`Link` never touches `aria-current` itself — its styling only reacts to the attribute being present, however\nit got there. That is what lets Angular Router own it entirely:"],[2978,"`Link` styles a native `<a>` as a navigation link while keeping every native and router semantic intact —\n`href`, `routerLink`, `target`, `aria-current`, and so on. Hover, active and focus rely on native\npseudo-classes; the current-page and disabled looks react directly to the `aria-current=\"page\"` and\n`aria-disabled` attributes, however they got set."],[2484,"`List` composes `@angular/aria/listbox`'s `Listbox` as a **host directive** on its own host element (not a\nwrapper), and `ListItem` composes `Option` the same way — required so a projected `ListItem` resolves its\nancestor `Listbox` through the element-injector hierarchy, which an `ng-content` wrapper would break. Every\nselection, keyboard-navigation and ARIA-reflection concern (`aria-selected`, `aria-disabled`, roving tabindex,\ntypeahead) comes from `@angular/aria`, not from galactik."],[2483,"`List` is a container of selectable rows built on `@angular/aria/listbox` (`role=\"listbox\"`/`role=\"option\"`).\n`ListItem` is a row, and a set of slot components/directives (`ListItemText`, `ListItemLeft`, `ListItemIcon`,\n`ListItemAvatar`, `ListItemAction`, `ListItemChevron`, `ListItemExpander`) compose the row content for content\nlists, action menus, navigation lists and tree-style rows. A row can also carry one **action** (typically\na delete) that a keyboard user reaches with a key rather than with Tab — see\nRow action and its key."],[2516,"`List`'s virtualizer estimates row height at a fixed value and never measures actual rendered height — keep\nrows a uniform height. This does not apply to `<aggregation-tree>`-style consumers with their own measured\nvirtualizer; a plain `List` always assumes uniform rows."],[2511,"`ListItemAction` renders a non-focusable `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">`, not a `<button>` — its host `<ListItem>`\nis already a listbox `option`, and ARIA forbids an option from containing an interactive control. The label\nyou pass renders in the hover tooltip, not as `aria-label`: the children of an `option` are presentational, and\na screen reader's mouse tracking (checked with NVDA) reads nothing under the pointer in an option, whatever\nrole or name the icon has. A named `role=\"img\"` was tried and changes nothing there."],[2499,"`ListItemAction`:"],[1496,"`LoadingComponent` and the `/loading` route pattern it was built for are no longer used. Authentication now\nhappens at bootstrap (`withBootstrapApp`/`signIn`), and `AuthGuard` redirects an unauthenticated user straight\nto the sign-in page — there is no longer an intermediate loading route to wait through. This component will be\nremoved in a future major release; do not use it in new code."],[1201,"`localizedNumber` — a number in the interface language"],[1676,"`location`"],[290,"`locked`"],[1064,"`login()` is not a question — for `oauth`/`saml` it navigates away to the identity provider. Ask\n`hasSession()` instead to decide what to render."],[1661,"`mail`"],[1865,"`margin` is a plain `number` of pixels, consumed directly in a `getBoundingClientRect()`-based calculation —\nnot a CSS length string. Pass `[margin]=\"8\"`, never `margin=\"8px\"`."],[2585,"`matchMedia` is not driveable in JSDOM — it matches nothing and cannot be told to change. Provide the service\ninstead of trying to steer the real one:"],[2859,"`md&#x60; &#x2A;(default)*"],[2786,"`Menu` applies two host directives rather than wrapping either: `@angular/aria`'s `Menu` (roving focus,\nkeyboard navigation, typeahead, the `itemSelected` output) and this library's own `PopoverDirective`\n(positioning, native show/close, the `state` signal). Selecting a non-submenu item closes the popover; opening\na submenu never bubbles into that same close path, so the root stays open while a submenu is showing."],[2795,"`MenuComponent` exposes its underlying `PopoverDirective` through the public `popover` property\n(`menu.popover.show()`/`.close()`/`.toggle()`/`.state()`), which is what makes this kind of imperative control\npossible."],[2788,"`MenuContent` accepts arbitrary projected content — group items visually with `role=\"group\"` + a heading span,\nseparated by a `.dialog-sep` marker div (both pre-styled)."],[2614,"`minSize`/`maxSize` per panel are honoured by both the pointer and the keyboard — what one panel refuses to\ntake, its neighbour keeps, so the total always stays at 100%. The handle implements the ARIA\nwindow-splitter pattern: `role=\"separator\"`,\n`aria-orientation` (the orientation of the *line*, not of the group — a separator between side-by-side panels\nis vertical), and `aria-valuenow`/`valuemin`/`valuemax` bounded by the panel before it. Arrow keys move the\nfocused handle by 10% of the group, along the axis the group's `direction` implies — a resize is therefore\nfully reachable without a pointer (WCAG 2.1.1). Dragging uses pointer events with pointer capture, so the drag\nfollows the pointer far outside the handle with no window-level listener, and a full-page overlay during the\ndrag keeps an `<iframe>` in a panel from swallowing the pointer."],[286,"`mode`"],[535,"`MultiSelectLabelsComponent` wraps a `galactik` `<Autocomplete>` — suggestions come from\n`client.api.labels.get(query, isPublic)`, and picking or removing a label calls\n`client.api.labels.add`/`remove` directly (not through `LabelService`, which exists mainly for the bulk and\nrights-checking operations used elsewhere)."],[819,"`name` and `column` identify which aggregation of the current query's result to render — `name` is matched\nfirst against the app's aggregation configuration, `column` as a fallback. Selecting an item and clicking\n**Apply** (or ticking a value in a facet search) writes the corresponding filter to the shared\n`QueryParamsStore` (and into the URL, unless `syncUrl` is `false`). It does **not** run the search: nothing in the\nlibrary re-runs the main query when the store changes, so the page must call `QueryService.search()` itself — see\nWho runs the search."],[1234,"`navigationEnd$` is the lower-level piece underneath it — an `Observable<NavigationEnd>` that also notifies\n`AuditService.notifyRouteChange()` once per distinct route (query-parameter-only changes on the same route,\nwhich make up most of a search session, do not re-notify)."],[1228,"`NavigationService` — the URL to return to after sign-in"],[1232,"`notifyDocument(type, doc, resultOrId, parameters?, rfmParameters?)` is the one most applications reach for\ndirectly — it also listens for the tab losing and regaining visibility right after the call, to notify a\n`Navigation_Return` event if the user opened the document and came back within a second. `notify()` is a no-op\nwhile the client reports no session (`client.auth.isAuthenticated()` is false)."],[212,"`number`"],[1039,"`number`"],[1038,"`offset`"],[1794,"`onConversionSelect` emits the selected `CConverter`, or `undefined` when none applies."],[1390,"`onConversionSelect` fires whenever the active conversion changes (including the very first auto-selection),\nso a parent can mirror it without owning any conversion state of its own."],[1274,"`onmessage()`/`onerror()` return RxJS `Observable`s rather than plain callbacks. Throws synchronously if\n`Worker` is unavailable in the current environment (a non-browser SSR context, most often)."],[1941,"`onSort` emits a `SortingChoice` (the configured choice plus a resolved `$isDesc` boolean) only when the user\npicks a **different** option than the one currently active — reselecting the active sort emits nothing."],[2981,"`openWindow` fully owns `target`/`rel` — the same way `disabled` owns `aria-disabled`/`tabindex` — and appends\nthe external-link icon automatically. Don't set `target`/`rel` manually on a link that binds it, and don't use\nit if a custom `rel` is needed (use a manual `link-icon-right` in that case instead)."],[1202,"`operator` — a legacy filter as a comparison string"],[1672,"`organisation`"],[1866,"`overflowManager` needs direct DOM access and cannot be used through `hostDirectives` — wrap it in an\n`ng-container` with an explicit `[target]` pointing at the real container element instead."],[1867,"`overflowManager` recounts when the container resizes or an individual item's size changes, but a Transloco\ntranslation load does not itself resize anything if it fires before layout — debounce\n`TranslocoService.events$` and call the directive's own `countItems()` (via `viewChild`) once the language\nactually finishes loading."],[1901,"`PagerComponent` still exists but lives under `components/deprecated/` and is not part of the pages this\nmigration documents — it predates `QueryService.gotoPage()`, which is the current, supported way to move\nbetween pages. See Query."],[2197,"`picked`/`removed` only report intent — the host is responsible for the backend call and for updating\n`selected` only on success."],[2078,"`PickWorkspace` opens the \"Save to workspace\" / \"Move to workspace\" picker. It offers only the workspaces the user can\nwrite to, and can make a new one on the spot. `<workspace-mention-list>` is the other way in: the workspaces as rows to\ntick, for a chat's \"+\" menu — with search, select all and a new-workspace button on request (see Mention workspaces)."],[227,"`placeholder`"],[2334,"`placeholder`, `size` and the built-in chevron only apply to the **default** trigger. Once you project a\n`[selectTrigger]`, you own the whole trigger element — size it, disable it and render its value yourself; none\nof the default-trigger inputs apply anymore."],[2926,"`PlaceholderClippedDirective` (`input[placeholderClipped]`, `exportAs=\"placeholderClipped\"`):"],[1035,"`Placement`"],[2819,"`PopoverDirective` wraps the native HTML **Popover API** (the `popover` attribute) with intelligent\npositioning from Floating UI. It responds to two selectors — `[popover]` and `[dropdown]` — which are the same\ndirective; `[dropdown]` only changes one default. Reach for it directly when the floating content is not a\nlist of actions; when it is, Menu is built on exactly this directive and adds the ARIA menu\nsemantics on top."],[207,"`popup`"],[188,"`popup=\"card\"` (default) floats under the field, as in a results header. `popup=\"inline\"` is flat, right under the field,\nout of the flow (opening it moves nothing) and 74px narrower than the field, as on a home page: it takes the place of what\nsits under the field, and `expandedChange` lets the host hide it (the filters) while the popup is open."],[215,"`popupFitScreen`"],[219,"`popupFlip`"],[211,"`popupOffset`"],[1034,"`position`"],[1233,"`post`/`get` both call the client's own `http` transport (`plugin/<method>`) rather than Angular's\n`HttpClient` — a plugin call issued this way is already covered by the client's own request/response handling\n(interface language, audit metadata, 401 recovery), with nothing further to register."],[1435,"`PreviewService` is what `<preview-content>` and `<preview-actions>` both delegate to: fetching the preview\ndata for a document, talking to the preview iframe through `postMessage`, and tracking zoom/page/highlight\nstate. Inject it directly only when building a preview surface that does not compose the existing components."],[293,"`PRINCIPAL_SEARCH_FILTER` takes a predicate over the suggestions — for instance `p => !p.isGroup` for users only."],[417,"`PrincipalService` is deprecated in favor of this store and will be removed in a future version — read\n`inject(PrincipalStore)` and the fields directly, or `getState(PrincipalStore)` for a plain snapshot, instead\nof subscribing to an `Observable`."],[413,"`PrincipalStore` holds the authenticated user's data — name, email, administrator status, password expiry —\nloaded once via `initialize()` and shared by every consumer through NgRx Signals."],[1727,"`privateCustomData` takes the same entries as `customData` (names, defaults, objects, `/`-separated paths, booleans,\narrays), and the form renders them the same way, in a card of their own, **Private**, after the others. What\nchanges is who can see them: only their owner. They are returned only for the signed-in user's own profile, and never\nby the `GET api/v2/user-profile/{id}` of another user."],[1683,"`profilePhoto`"],[283,"`provideAccessSwitch()` adds a switch that adds or removes one reader value. `visible` is a boolean, or a\npredicate that receives the logged-in user. `label` and `description` go through Transloco: a translation key is\ntranslated, a literal text is shown as is."],[1101,"`provideAtomicClient()` alone is enough at the application root, where the root singletons already resolve to\nthe right client. A **route or component subtree that targets another backend** needs more: its own client\n*and* its own copy of everything that holds per-backend state. `provideAtomicScope()` provides both."],[1061,"`provideAtomicClient(config)` registers a factory for `ATOMIC_CLIENT` that calls\n`createAtomicClient(config)` from `@sinequa/atomic` — one client, one configuration, one session, one set of\nevent subscribers, isolated from whatever the default client is doing. `injectAtomicClient()` is\n`inject(ATOMIC_CLIENT)` under a clearer name."],[1917,"`provideAtomicNotifications()` (see Notify failed requests as\ntoasts) surfaces a failed request to the *user* as a toast — only for\nHTTP statuses `400`, `403`, `500` and `503`, and only if your application renders the `notification` event — but that leaves the *component* still rendering \"0 results\" underneath unless it also checks\n`$error` itself. The two are complementary, not either/or."],[605,"`provideAtomicNotifications()` does the same job for the Sinequa\nclient's **own** requests, by listening to its `requestFailed` event — a different channel entirely. The two\ncover disjoint request sets (the client's, versus `HttpClient` requests from `@sinequa/agent`,\n`@sinequa/assistant` or your own code), so registering both raises exactly one toast per failure, never two."],[1088,"`provideAtomicNotifications()` registered at the root only covers the root client's events. A\nscope serving a second backend has its own event subscribers — register\n`provideAtomicNotifications()` in the scope's own providers too if you want its failures notified."],[1081,"`provideAtomicNotifications()` subscribes to the client's own `requestFailed` event and raises a toast for the\nstatuses worth telling the user about. It does not replace `toastInterceptorFn`: the two serve disjoint channels\n(see the first pitfall below)."],[1114,"`provideAtomicScope()` deliberately returns a plain `Provider[]`, not `EnvironmentProviders` — a component's\n`providers` is typed `Provider[]` and rejects `EnvironmentProviders`. `Route.providers`,\n`ApplicationConfig.providers` and `createEnvironmentInjector` all accept\n`Array<Provider | EnvironmentProviders>` and flatten nested arrays, so the routing form works either way; only\nthe component form depends on this."],[2720,"`provideCallable()` — registered once at bootstrap (`providers: [provideCallable()]`) — captures the root\n`Injector` so `Callable.call(props)` works without passing `{ injector }` explicitly. Without it, and with no\n`injector` passed in `options`, `call()` throws synchronously."],[59,"`provideCallable()` must be registered once in your application's providers for `ConfirmSave.call(...)` to\nwork without passing an explicit injector — see the Dialog page's Options\nsection."],[1159,"`provideDocumentLang()` keeps `<html lang>` on the active Transloco language (RGAA 8.4 / WCAG 3.1.1), whichever\ncontrol changed it — the user menus, the user-profile form, a host dialog."],[2584,"`provideInputModality()` starts an `InputModalityService` that tells how the page is being driven — `modality()` is\n`\"pointer\"`, `\"touch\"` or `\"keyboard\"` — and, while it is `\"touch\"`, sets `--stroke-focus` to `transparent` on `<html>`.\nEvery galactik ring is painted with that variable, so a tap on a field no longer leaves a ring that means nothing to someone\nwithout a keyboard. A navigation key (`Tab`, the arrows, `Home`, `End`, `PageUp`, `PageDown`, `Escape`), or any key pressed with Ctrl, Cmd or Option (a shortcut of the application: the on-screen keyboard has no such key), gives the rings\nback, which a tablet with a keyboard attached does at its first `Tab`; the on-screen keyboard has none of those keys, so\ntyping in a field never brings them back. `data-input-modality` is written on `<html>` for your own CSS."],[1246,"`provideSessionExpiry()` raises a warning toast — with an \"Extend session\" action — shortly before the session ends, and an error toast — with a\n\"Sign in again\" action — when the backend says it has. Both are `notify` events, rendered by your application's\ntoast bridge."],[1142,"`provideStartupAggregation()` is a shortcut for one `STARTUP_DATA_SOURCE` — a function returning a\n`Promise<StartupData>` that `ApplicationService` calls on every initialization: at start-up, and again after\nthe sign-in form or a password change. Provide the token yourself to read the data some other way: here, from\na JSON block the server inlined into `index.html`, which costs no request at all. The same rules apply: a part\nmarked `{ ok: false }`, or missing, is read the usual way, and a source that throws makes the start-up fall back\nto the parallel reads."],[1146,"`provideStartupAggregation(endpoint?)` takes the endpoint's path on the backend, `\"bff/bootstrap\"` by default.\nTo read the start-up data some other way, provide `STARTUP_DATA_SOURCE` with a function returning a\n`Promise<StartupData>`."],[2437,"`pulse` wraps the dot in an expanding, fading halo for a live \"right now\" status — a `::before` on the dot\nitself, not a second element, so it stays a single boolean to toggle:"],[1221,"`queryNameResolver` reads `AppStore.getDefaultQuery()` and resolves to its `name`, or an empty string if the\napplication has no default query configured yet — it never throws, so a route that resolves it does not need\nto guard against a missing application configuration on its own."],[864,"`QueryParamsStore` keeps **one filter entry per field or per aggregation** — three selected authors are a\nsingle `values` array, and two selected date buckets are a nested `or` tree. Rendering that directly would\nshow one chip for three choices, and removing it would drop all three. `<applied-filters>` expands each entry\ninto one chip per value first, so each one can be removed independently."],[1914,"`QueryService` is the one place a search actually happens: it turns a `Query` into a request, exposes the last\n`Result` and its failure state as signals, and moves between pages. Almost every other component in this\nsection — sorting, spelling correction, missing terms — reads the `Result` this service produced rather than\nissuing a request of its own."],[30,"`QueryService` runs the search and exposes the result as a signal; `<Aggregation>` renders one facet from that\nsame result and turns a click into an applied filter."],[1046,"`QuickFilter`"],[2258,"`Radio` wraps a native `<input type=\"radio\">` inside an accessible `<label>`, meant to be used in groups of two\nor more sharing the same `name` for a single exclusive choice. There is no `RadioGroup` container — exclusivity\ncomes from giving every radio the same `name` and deriving each one's `checked` from one shared signal."],[2608,"`ResizableHandle`"],[2607,"`ResizablePanel`"],[2611,"`ResizablePanelGroup` lays a row (or column) of panels out and lets the user move the boundary between two of\nthem, by dragging the handle or by arrowing it. Three components work together: `ResizablePanelGroup` (the\nflex container and single owner of the sizes), `ResizablePanel` (one region, sized as a share of the group)\nand `ResizableHandle` (the divider, focusable and draggable)."],[2387,"`resize=\"auto\"` is a progressive enhancement: the CSS `field-sizing: content` property ships in Chrome/Edge\n123+ and Safari 17.4+, but not in Firefox, so the drag handle is only removed inside an `@supports` guard —\napplying it unconditionally would leave Firefox with a box that neither grows nor can be dragged."],[2204,"`resource.value()` throws in the error state. `Autocomplete` reads it behind `hasValue()`, so a failing `loader`\nshows an alert row (`errorText`) instead of crashing — nothing to guard on the consumer side."],[2934,"`role=\"tooltip\"` and `aria-describedby` while open"],[1225,"`ROUTE_COMPONENTS` — registering components for dynamic routes"],[2546,"`rowActivate` fires on `Enter` because the interactive cell sits above the projected content in the DOM.\n`endApproached` fires once keyboard focus reaches the last `rowsBeforeEnd` rows, so an infinite-scroll list can\nkeep growing for a keyboard user who never touches the mouse wheel."],[2529,"`RowFeed` renders a list as a WAI-ARIA feed: a `role=\"feed\"` container of `<article>`s. A grid without headers\nreads as a layout table (RGAA 5.3), so use it instead of `RowGrid` for a list of cards filled\nin as the user scrolls. Article content is fully projected through an `ng-template`."],[2544,"`RowGrid` is a keyboard-navigable ARIA grid shell for a list of rows: `↑`/`↓` walk the rows, `Tab` enters the\nfocused row's own controls, `Enter` activates the row. Row content is fully projected, so it fits any row\nshape a consumer needs. Reach for it instead of `List`/`ListItem` whenever a row holds more than\none focusable control — a listbox `option` cannot contain one at all."],[2388,"`rows` has no effect once `field-sizing: content` applies, so `minRows`/`maxRows` exist precisely to bound an\nauto-sizing field — the sizing API that survives where `rows` no longer does."],[248,"`saved`"],[173,"`SavedSearchesComponent.onDelete` deletes by the **index into the raw `userSettingsStore.savedSearches()`\narray** (`ListPanelDeleteEvent.index`), not by any value comparison — this is handled correctly by the\ncomponent itself, but a custom list built the same way must delete by that same index, not by matching on\n`url` or `label` (two saved searches can legitimately share a display label)."],[165,"`SavedSearchesService` stores each saved search as its full URL (`SearchItem.url`), not as a structured filter\nobject. Reopening one means navigating to that URL — `navigateToSearch()` parses it back into query params — so\na saved search stays valid across app versions as long as the URL scheme itself does."],[172,"`SavedSearchPopover.savedSearch` recomputes from `queryText()` — confirm the same text that was passed to\n`SavedSearch.call(...)` is also what the popover's `[queryText]` input carries afterwards. A popover bound to a\nsignal that changed shape between the two (trimmed differently, say) will not find a match even though the\nsearch was genuinely saved."],[1026,"`scheme`"],[2871,"`screen`"],[1916,"`search()` still returns an `Observable<Result>` for callers that want to compose it, but the same `Result`\nalso lands on the `result` signal (and `failure`, `result().$error`) — which is what a template reads instead\nof subscribing manually. **The observable never errors**: a failed request still emits, because throwing would\ncancel the navigation of any route resolver built on this service, which historically rendered an empty page\ninstead."],[2277,"`search` is emitted with the current `value()`, always a `string`, in these cases:"],[2290,"`searchBarVariants` styles the disabled state with `has-disabled:` (`:has(:disabled)`), which matches **any**\ndisabled descendant — including your own projected content — and greys out the entire pill. Use `aria-disabled`\nplus `pointer-events-none opacity-50` and guard the handler yourself instead of the native `disabled` attribute\non projected content."],[242,"`searched`"],[2301,"`SearchInput` reuses `InputGroup`'s visual system (icon slots, border/focus/disabled states) and\nadds only a clear (×) button — no send button, no pill shape. For a self-contained search field with its own\nsubmit control, see `SearchBar` instead."],[235,"`searchInputText`"],[249,"`SearchItem \\| undefined`"],[223,"`searchPath`"],[1822,"`selectCorrected()` and `selectOriginal()` are the two methods every message's links call — both patch\n`QueryParamsStore` with `spellingCorrectionMode: \"dymonly\"` and navigate with the chosen text, so the engine\ndoes not re-correct the choice the user just made."],[3030,"`selectedTab` on `TabList` sets the initially active tab; selection then becomes user-driven, or bind\n`[(selectedTab)]` for full two-way control from the host."],[3031,"`selectionMode=\"follow\"` (default) activates a tab as soon as focus reaches it — best for stateless content.\n`selectionMode=\"explicit\"` only highlights the tab on focus; the user must press `Space`/`Enter` to activate\nit — best when activation is expensive (a network fetch, a heavy render)."],[117,"`SelectionStore` holds two independent things: the single currently-**open** article (`article`), and the\n**multi-selection** (`multiSelection`, an `Article[]`) the toolbar acts on."],[2616,"`setLayout()` is ignored unless the array covers exactly the panels present — a partial layout would leave the\ngroup summing to something other than 100%."],[2094,"`ShareWorkspace` opens the \"Manage access\" dialog for one workspace. `<workspace-detail>` opens it from its\n\"Manage access\" button; call it yourself from anywhere else."],[2883,"`Sheet` invents no default accessible name. Project a `DialogTitle` into `SheetHeader`, or set `ariaLabel` (or\n`labelledby`) — one of the three is required for an accessible sheet, and this is the single most common way\nto ship one that is not."],[2887,"`SheetBody` is `overflow-y-auto`. When the body holds a list that scrolls by itself (a panel also used inline, with\n`overflow-y-auto` on its list), the list only overflows if its wrapper has the height of the body. Without it the\nlist grows to its content, the body scrolls instead, and two scrollbars show. Make the body inert and the wrapper fill it:"],[2877,"`SheetFooter` is a right-aligned action row on a tinted band, pinned below the scrollable body. Every close\npath — a footer button, the header's close button, `Escape`, a scrim click — lands on the same `closed` output."],[2706,"`show()` — non-modal. On a real `<dialog>` tag this has **no** backdrop."],[2707,"`showModal()` — modal, native `<dialog>` semantics (native focus trap, native `Escape`) when applied to a\nreal `<dialog>` tag."],[796,"`showModal()` closes any native auto popover that is not an ancestor of the dialog, so a calendar mounted\noutside the popover took it down with it. `<aggregation-date>` mounts the dialog inside itself, which keeps it\na descendant of whatever popover hosts the facet. If it still happens, the facet is probably not rendered in\nthe popover you think it is: check that the facet element really is inside the `[popover]` element in the DOM."],[2734,"`showModal()` closes every native `popover=\"auto\"` that is not an **ancestor** of the dialog. A callable dialog\nis mounted in `document.body`, so it is never one: opening it from inside a popover hides that popover — and\ndestroys whatever it rendered, the caller and the dialog with it. The symptom is a dialog that flashes and\ndisappears, or never seems to open."],[946,"`showMoreFiltersButton` might be `false`, but if not: the overflow count is measured against the **full**\nauthorized list, not the list capped by `filtersCount` — so this should not happen. If it does, check that\n`aggregations`/`includeFilters`/`excludeFilters` are consistent between what you expect and what\n`getAuthorizedFilters` actually resolves; the \"More\" button and the bar always agree with each other, but not\nnecessarily with an assumption made outside the component."],[2708,"`showPopover()` — the native Popover API, light-dismiss."],[203,"`showSave`"],[1389,"`showSearchActions` reaches all the way down to `<preview-actions>`'s own `showSearch` input — set it once here\nrather than wrapping the nested component yourself."],[2852,"`side` is what distinguishes a sheet from a dialog: it drives the inset, the size axis (a **width** for\n`left`/`right`, a **height** for `top`/`bottom`), the border, the rounded corners (the two corners flush\nagainst the viewport stay square) and the slide direction, all from the one input."],[2996,"`SIDEBAR_REF`"],[2999,"`Sidebar` is an app navigation panel: collapsible to icons on desktop, a native `<dialog>` drawer\n(`Sheet`) below the mobile breakpoint. It covers the common case — no rail, no nested\nsubmenus, no skeleton loading — with a real focusable trigger, a genuinely modal mobile drawer, and item labels\nthat stay in the accessibility tree once the panel goes icon-only."],[3000,"`Sidebar` renders as a native `<aside>` on desktop, and swaps to a `Sheet` anchored to\n`side()` below the 768px breakpoint (via `BreakpointObserverService`)\n— projecting the exact same `sidebarHeader`/`sidebarContent`/`sidebarFooter` content either way, nothing to\nbranch on in application code."],[2995,"`SidebarTriggerDirective`"],[3010,"`SidebarTriggerDirective` also reflects `aria-expanded` on its host, bound to the resolved sidebar's\n`expanded()` signal — a screen reader announces whether activating it opens or closes the panel, whichever\nviewport applies."],[454,"`SignedOut` is the post-logout confirmation shown on the `/logout` route by\n`AuthPageComponent`. It states that the session has ended and offers a single\nexplicit action to sign in again."],[334,"`signIn()` per mode"],[439,"`SignInComponent` (`<sign-in>`) takes `redirectAfterSuccess` too (forwarded to the form), plus:"],[431,"`SignInComponent` only ever renders the credentials form. Route `/login` (and `/logout`) to\r\n`AuthPageComponent` instead — it switches between sign-in, change-password, forgot-password and signed-out\r\nbased on the route and the `mode` query param. See Authentication flows."],[437,"`SignInFormComponent` (`<sign-in-form>`):"],[199,"`size`"],[2853,"`size`"],[2249,"`size`/`scheme` are not linked automatically between `InputGroup` and `InputCounterComponent` — their CVAs are\nindependent. Mirror both explicitly on the counter, as in the recipe above."],[1679,"`skills`"],[2856,"`sm`"],[2640,"`sortable.items()` is the order **shown**: the list you gave, or, while an item is dragged, the order a drop would\ngive. Render from it, not from your own list, and the other items make room for the dragged one as the pointer moves.\nThe list you gave only changes when you adopt what `(reorder)` reports."],[2639,"`SortableDirective` (`[sortable]`) makes the items of a list reorderable by drag and drop, and from the keyboard with\nAlt + ↑ / ↓. `SortableItemDirective` (`[sortableItem]`) marks each element. The\ndirective owns the gesture, not the data: it reports the new order through `(reorder)`, and the consumer keeps — and\nsaves — the list."],[2646,"`sortableDisabled` removes `draggable` and ignores the shortcut — for a list the user may not reorder right now."],[2647,"`sortableItemDisabled` keeps one item from being moved, by the pointer or the keyboard, while the others stay free.\nThey may still be dropped before or after it: a disabled item is not moved by the user, but its position shifts to\nmake room. It carries `data-disabled`: style it so that it is easy to tell from the others — a flat neutral\nbackground, muted text, no hover, and no handle if the items have one."],[1216,"`SourceIconPipe` is `@deprecated` and kept only for backward compatibility — it returns a `fa-*` class string\nlooked up against `AppStore.sources()`, a workplace-search-only shape most applications' custom JSON does not\npopulate, in which case it silently falls back to `\"far fa-file\"`. Do not reach for it in new code; prefer a\nsource-specific icon resolved from the document's own metadata instead."],[1115,"`storagePrefix` only namespaces the keys the client writes in **Web Storage**, which holds the CSRF token. It does\nnot isolate the session itself: the JSON Web Token lives in an `HttpOnly` cookie, and a browser keys a cookie by\n(domain, path, name). Two backends reached through the same host, with the same cookie name and path, therefore\noverwrite each other's session cookie whatever the prefixes say — signing in to one logs the other out."],[224,"`string`"],[228,"`string`"],[236,"`string`"],[243,"`string`"],[246,"`string`"],[1660,"`string`"],[1663,"`string`"],[1666,"`string`"],[1670,"`string`"],[1674,"`string`"],[1678,"`string`"],[1681,"`string`"],[1685,"`string`"],[2803,"`SubMenu`'s host relies on a `data-[visible=false]:hidden` utility to hide itself when closed. Overriding\n`class` with a `display` utility (`flex`, `block`, …) on `<SubMenu>` drops that variant unless you keep it or\nan equivalent rule — the panel then lays out inline even while closed."],[1818,"`suggestion`"],[231,"`suggestionsProvider`"],[2677,"`SwipeAction` is inert with a mouse, so it can wrap the row on every device. What changes under a finger is the visible\nbutton, which the swipe replaces:"],[2345,"`Switch` wraps a native `<input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"switch\">` inside an accessible `<label>`, styled as an\non/off track-and-thumb toggle with a built-in check/cross icon in the thumb."],[1198,"`syslang` — a translated system string"],[3028,"`Tabs` is a thin styling layer over Angular's official ARIA tabs primitives (`@angular/aria/tabs`).\n`TabsComponent`, `TabListComponent`, `TabComponent` and `TabPanelComponent` each apply the matching\n`@angular/aria/tabs` directive as a host directive — roles, focus management, keyboard navigation and ARIA\nattributes are entirely delegated to `@angular/aria`; galactik only contributes visual variants and classes."],[3036,"`TabsComponent` applies **no default layout classes at all** — unlike `atomic-ui`'s `Tabs`. Always add\n`class=\"flex flex-col\"` (stacked) or `class=\"flex flex-row\"` (side by side) yourself."],[2559,"`Tag` categorizes content read-only — a document type, an applied filter shown for reference, a status label.\nIt is a bare directive with no template: it only contributes host classes to whichever tag you write it on,\nprojecting its content as-is."],[2566,"`Tag` has no click handler, no `disabled` state, and no built-in remove/close button — it is read-only by\ndesign. For a removable tag list backed by search/selection state, reach for `Autocomplete`, which renders its\napplied values as `Tag`s internally and exposes the removal outputs; don't try to bolt dismiss behavior onto a\nbare `Tag`."],[2567,"`Tag` uses `variant` itself for solid/outline styling (`primary`/`secondary`) and has no separate `fill`\ninput. `Badge` (see Badge) instead uses `variant` for its icon/number discriminator and a\nseparate `fill` for solid/tonal styling. The two components' `variant` values name unrelated things."],[2361,"`TagInput` collects a list of short free-text labels — keywords, categories, e-mail addresses — in one field. The tags\nsit inside the field, before the text being typed. It reuses `InputGroup`'s visual system (border,\nfocus, disabled and error states) and shows each tag as a `Tag` with a remove button."],[2363,"`TagInput` is a signal forms `FormValueControl<string[]>`: bind it with `[(value)]`, or as a form field."],[2562,"`TagInput`, the principal picker's chips and `Autocomplete`'s chips all use it. It is an element with its own button, not an\nattribute on yours: a component that imports `ButtonComponent` (which matches every `button` of its template) would\notherwise restyle it."],[2386,"`TextareaGroup` is the multi-line sibling of `InputGroup` — same state model, same scheme\npalette, same counter typography — plus a `resize` strategy for how its height behaves."],[1459,"`TextChunkService` retrieves text chunks — a highlight location plus a configurable number of sentences of\ncontext on either side — for a given document. Used where a preview needs to show highlighted passages without\nloading the whole converted document."],[1545,"`ThemeStore.scopes()` has no entry for that scope — `toggleDarkMode()` reads the current scope and returns\nearly when it is `undefined` or has no `cssVars`. Register the scope first (`loadDefaultTheme`/`setCurrentTheme`),\nsame as ThemeProvider."],[1525,"`ThemeStore.scopes()` has no entry for that scope yet — the directive's `effect()` returns early when the\nscope is unregistered. Call `loadDefaultTheme(scope)` or `setCurrentTheme(scope, name)` once before (or\nreactively alongside) mounting the element."],[1104,"`ThemeStore` is deliberately absent from that list: the theme is a user-interface preference, not backend\nstate, and both scopes should keep sharing it."],[604,"`toastInterceptorFn` watches the Angular `HttpClient` channel and raises a toast (through `@sinequa/atomic`'s\nnotification system) for the handful of error statuses a user should actually hear about — leaving every other\nfailure to whatever already handles it."],[2172,"`Toggle` (standalone) and `Toggle[value]` (grouped item) are two distinct components sharing the `Toggle`/\n`toggle` tag name, discriminated by the presence of the `value` attribute. Grouped items require an ancestor\n`<ToggleGroup>` — its selection model comes entirely from `@angular/aria/toolbar` (roving tabindex, arrow keys,\ntypeahead, all free)."],[2171,"`Toggle` is a pill-shaped pressed/unpressed button. Used alone it is a self-contained two-way `[(pressed)]`\ncontrol; nested inside a `ToggleGroup` it becomes an item of a segmented control — `single` (exclusive, like a\nradio group) or `multi` (independent selections)."],[2937,"`tooltip-delay` (delay before hiding)"],[2941,"`tooltip-duration`, default 3000"],[2939,"`tooltip-offset`"],[2935,"`tooltip-position`"],[2908,"`TooltipDirective` (`[tooltip]`) shows a short text, or a template, next to the element it sits on. It opens on\nhover **and** on keyboard focus, closes on `Escape`, on blur and on scroll, and lets the pointer move onto the\ntooltip without closing it."],[2940,"`tooltipOffset` (the old name still works)"],[2936,"`tooltipPosition` (the old name still works)"],[2855,"`top` / `bottom` (height)"],[1199,"`translocoDate` — a locale-aware `DatePipe`"],[2501,"`trigger()` is also public: it does what a click on the icon does, unless the action is disabled. `ListItem` calls it\non the key."],[205,"`true`"],[217,"`true` for `size=\"hero\"` with `popup=\"inline\"`, `false` otherwise"],[221,"`true`, `false` for `size=\"hero\"`"],[2924,"`TruncatedDirective` (`[truncated]`, `exportAs=\"truncated\"`):"],[2920,"`TruncatedDirective` cannot measure a placeholder: it is not content of the field, so the `scrollWidth` of an `<input>`\nsays nothing about it. `PlaceholderClippedDirective` (`input[placeholderClipped]`) measures the text with the font of the\nfield and compares it with the room the field leaves it. `isClipped()` is exposed through `exportAs=\"placeholderClipped\"`."],[2112,"`UploadDocuments.call()` without an injector needs `provideCallable()` from `@sinequa/galactik` in the providers.\nThe dialog refuses to open for someone who cannot write to the workspace and ends with `dialog-cancel`."],[2135,"`uploadEndpoint` defaults to an empty string, which is no endpoint. Give the tus endpoint of your backend — a\nrelative path is resolved against the configured `backendUrl`."],[1648,"`UserProfileFormComponent` can also be embedded directly (`<user-profile-form />`, no inputs — it resolves the\nprofile itself from `PrincipalStore` and `UserProfileService`) when a host wants it inline rather than in a\ndialog."],[1646,"`UserProfileFormComponent` renders and edits the current user's profile; the `Settings` callable dialog\nhosts it in its Account panel. The form edits one property at a time — each row is read-only until its edit button is\npressed, and saving sends a `PATCH` for that single property only, never the whole profile."],[1757,"`UserProfileFormComponent` renders its own `<form>`, and so does the `ChangePassword` view it hosts inline.\nNever wrap it in a `<form>` of your own — nested `<form>` elements are invalid HTML, and the outer one's submit\nbehavior becomes unpredictable."],[1750,"`UserProfileFormComponent` takes no input and has no output — everything else is protected, internal wiring."],[1767,"`UserSettingsStore` holds the parts of a user's experience that outlive one session — recent searches,\nbookmarks, saved searches, baskets, alerts, language and assistant preferences — and keeps them synced with the\nbackend on every mutation."],[2560,"`variant` (`primary` soft fill / `secondary` outline) is crossed with `scheme` (12 semantic palettes). Having\nno template means a `Tag` composes with anything projected inside it — plain text, or an icon alongside a\nlabel, sized automatically through the `.tag-icon` slot class:"],[585,"`variant` and `size` are forwarded to the underlying galactik button, so they accept its values."],[123,"`variant=\"floating\"` is the lexiQ layout: no bar, but detached galactik buttons in the bottom-right corner of\nthe window — \"Add to collection\" (accent), \"Export\" and the optional AI action (secondary). It shows no count:\nthe page that renders the checkboxes already does."],[3005,"`variant=\"inset\"` draws no border or fill on `Sidebar` itself; the encased look comes from `sidebarInset` on\nthe app's main content wrapper instead, which supplies the surface. `variant=\"floating\"` needs no companion\nelement — it detaches with a margin, border and shadow of its own, and reserves **no** layout space, ever,\nfloating on top of whatever sibling content follows it."],[2153,"`variant=\"none\"` short-circuits the whole variant computation and returns `class()` alone — `size`, `iconOnly`\nand `scheme` are silently ignored whenever `variant` is `\"none\"`. For an icon-only button, keep a real variant\n(commonly `\"tertiary\"`) together with `[iconOnly]=\"true\"`; never rely on `variant=\"none\"` to get a bare icon\nbutton."],[2422,"`variant=\"number\"` gives breathing-room padding and a minimum width equal to the row height, so it grows into\na pill past one or two digits. `variant=\"icon\"` forces a square aspect ratio, meant for a single icon child.\nNeither variant carries any positioning of its own — gluing a badge onto another element (a status dot on an\n`Avatar`, an unread count on a tab) is your own `relative`/`absolute` wrapper:"],[1269,"`withFetch` — a fetch wrapper with baseline error handling"],[1560,"`withThemeBodyHook()` only ever targets `document.body`, for the one scope you passed it (`\"application\"` by\ndefault). Every other scope needs its own `[themeProvider]=\"scopeName\"` on the element that should carry it —\nregistering a scope in `ThemeStore` does not apply it anywhere on its own."],[1557,"`withThemes()` pushes your themes into a module-level array read by `ThemeStore` — it does nothing else, and\norder relative to `withThemeBodyHook()` matters: the hook reads that array through `setCurrentTheme()`, so it\nmust run after `withThemes()` in the bootstrap chain. `withThemeBodyHook()` is the one integration that targets\n`document.body` directly, through the fixed scope name `\"application\"` (or whatever `scope` you pass it) —\nevery other scope is applied through `[themeProvider]` on a specific element instead (see\nThemeProvider)."],[2132,"`workspaceId` is what is shown. `null` is the list; an id is that workspace, with its documents. It is a two-way\nmodel, so a host can open a workspace and is told which one is open."],[2050,"`WorkspaceMentionRows` (the default rows), `WORKSPACE_MENTION` and `injectWorkspaceMentionScope()` are exported: a host can provide the scope itself\nor reuse the rows. The rows fill the height they are given; `--workspace-mention-rows-max-height` (18rem by default) caps them."],[1983,"`WorkspacesShell` is the whole view in one component. Every part it is made of is exported too, for a host that\nwants its own layout. None of the parts navigates or opens a dialog it does not own: each one *says* what the user\nasked for, and whoever hosts it decides."],[2035,"`ws.items()` are the rows the search, the category and the predicate leave, each with its `id`, `name`, `description`, `color`,\n`documentCount` and whether it is `selected`."],[2865,"`xl`"],[54,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;@angular/core&#x22;: { type: &#x22;^22.0.0&#x22;, description: &#x22;The library's own version.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;@angular/aria&#x22;: { type: &#x22;^22.0.0&#x22;, description: &#x22;Accessible interaction primitives several components build on (List, Tabs, Toolbar…).&#x22; },\n  cn: { type: &#x22;^0.2.5&#x22;, description: &#x22;The class-merging helper every component's `class` input goes through.&#x22; },\n}\"\n/>"],[1970,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;<lexiq-workspaces>&#x22;: {\n    type: &#x22;attributes&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;upload-endpoint, query-name, theme (light or dark), workspace-id. Methods: showList(), showDetail(id), addWorkspace().&#x22;,\n  },\n  &#x22;<lexiq-workspace-picker>&#x22;: {\n    type: &#x22;methods&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;openSaveCard(anchor, { documents }) and openMoveMenu(anchor, documentIds, { fromWorkspaceId }). Events: save (detail: { name }) and moved.&#x22;,\n  },\n  &#x22;<lexiq-workspace-mention-list>&#x22;: {\n    type: &#x22;attributes, method and property&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;searchable, select-all, allow-create, search-fields (name or name+description), and the selectedIds property to own the ticks. Method selectById(id), and items (the rows, for a host that draws its own menu). Events: selection-changed (detail: { ids, count }) and workspace-created (detail: the workspace).&#x22;,\n  },\n}\"\n/>"],[3009,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;aria-label&#x22;: { type: &#x22;string | undefined&#x22;, description: &#x22;Accessible name of the button — usually its only one, being icon-only. No default.&#x22; },\n  sidebar: { type: &#x22;SidebarRef | undefined&#x22;, description: &#x22;The <Sidebar> to control, when this button isn't projected inside one.&#x22; },\n}\"\n/>"],[2350,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;aria-label&#x22;: { type: &#x22;string | undefined&#x22;, description: &#x22;Accessible name of the inner input.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;aria-labelledby&#x22;: { type: &#x22;string | undefined&#x22;, description: &#x22;Id of the element that names the inner input; takes precedence over aria-label.&#x22; },\n  checked: { type: &#x22;boolean&#x22;, default: &#x22;false&#x22;, description: &#x22;Two-way bindable on/off state.&#x22; },\n  size: { type: '&#x22;small&#x22; | &#x22;xsmall&#x22; | &#x22;xs&#x22;', default: '&#x22;small&#x22;', description: '&#x22;xs&#x22; is identical to &#x22;xsmall&#x22;.' },\n  disabled: { type: &#x22;boolean&#x22;, default: &#x22;false&#x22;, description: &#x22;Disables the control; usable as a bare attribute.&#x22; },\n  compact: { type: &#x22;boolean&#x22;, default: &#x22;false&#x22;, description: &#x22;Drops the 44×44 touch target: the hitbox shrinks to the track. For a switch inside a row that is already the touch target.&#x22; },\n  presentational: { type: &#x22;boolean&#x22;, default: &#x22;false&#x22;, description: &#x22;Decorative state indicator inside a parent control: not focusable, hidden from assistive technology, implies compact.&#x22; },\n}\"\n/>"],[2565,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;aria-label&#x22;: { type: &#x22;string&#x22;, description: &#x22;Required. What the button removes (\\&#x22;Remove design\\&#x22;): an icon alone names nothing. Nothing in the library translates, so pass it translated and bind it as [aria-label].&#x22; },\n  disabled: { type: &#x22;boolean&#x22;, default: &#x22;false&#x22;, description: &#x22;The button stays in place and removes nothing.&#x22; },\n  removed: { type: &#x22;void&#x22;, description: &#x22;Output. Fires on a click, Enter or Space. The click does not bubble, so a handler on the field around the tags is not triggered.&#x22; },\n}\"\n/>"],[347,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;auth.recoverFromUnauthorized&#x22;: { type: &#x22;wired by bootstrapApp&#x22;, description: &#x22;Every request @sinequa/atomic issues on its own transport.&#x22; },\n  authInterceptorFn: { type: &#x22;HttpInterceptorFn&#x22;, description: &#x22;Every request built with Angular's HttpClient — @sinequa/agent, @sinequa/assistant, your own code.&#x22; },\n}\"\n/>"],[1276,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;buildQuery(partial?)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;Partial<Query>&#x22;, description: &#x22;Merges URL params, route queryName, and partial into a complete Query.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;getQueryNameFromRoute()&#x22;: { type: &#x22;() => string | undefined&#x22;, description: &#x22;Walks the live ActivatedRoute tree for a queryName.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;getCurrentPath()&#x22;: { type: &#x22;() => string | undefined&#x22;, description: &#x22;The current ActivatedRoute snapshot's URL segments, joined.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;getCurrentQueryName()&#x22;: { type: &#x22;() => string | undefined&#x22;, description: &#x22;Matches the current path against the static Router.config.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;new InlineWorker(fn)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(fn: () => void) => InlineWorker&#x22;, description: &#x22;fn's body becomes the whole worker script — no closures.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;withFetch(callback, injector?)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(() => Promise<T>, injector?) => Promise<T | undefined>&#x22;, description: &#x22;undefined on any error; injector enables 401 → sign-in.&#x22; },\n}\"\n/>"],[311,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;client.config.loginPath&#x22;: { type: &#x22;string&#x22;, default: '&#x22;/login&#x22;', description: &#x22;Where to redirect when authentication is needed.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;client.config.authMode&#x22;: {\n    type: &#x22;AuthMode&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;The source of truth for the authentication method — branch on authMode.kind, not the legacy useCredentials/useSSO booleans.&#x22;,\n  },\n}\"\n/>"],[1782,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;getFromSuggestQueriesForText(text)&#x22;: {\n    type: &#x22;(text: string): Promise<Suggestion[][]>&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;One array per configured suggest query — flatten to merge them, or keep them grouped.&#x22;,\n  },\n  &#x22;getFromUserSettingsForText(text, maxCount)&#x22;: {\n    type: &#x22;(text: string, maxCount: number | Autocomplete): Suggestion[]&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;Synchronous. Grouped by recent-searches, saved-searches, bookmarks.&#x22;,\n  },\n}\"\n/>"],[1751,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;getUserProfile(userId)&#x22;: {\n    type: &#x22;(userId: Signal<string | undefined>): ResourceRef<UserProfile | undefined>&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;UserProfileService. A signal of undefined issues no request. The resource is shared by every caller and created with the service's own injector, so it outlives the dialog that first asked for it.&#x22;,\n  },\n}\"\n/>"],[1480,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;goHome()&#x22;: { type: &#x22;() => void&#x22;, description: 'Navigates to &#x22;/&#x22;, then reloads the page.' },\n  message: { type: &#x22;string | undefined&#x22;, description: 'Read once from the &#x22;message&#x22; query param at construction; undefined shows no detail box.' },\n}\"\n/>"],[1213,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;highlightWord: (word: string, clipBy?: number)&#x22;: {\n    type: &#x22;HighlightWords.Chunk[]&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;word to highlight, clipBy optionally limits the returned text's length.&#x22;,\n  },\n  &#x22;syslang: (lang?: string)&#x22;: {\n    type: &#x22;string | null&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;lang forces a language regardless of the current interface language.&#x22;,\n  },\n  &#x22;translocoDate: (format?: string, timezone?: string)&#x22;: {\n    type: &#x22;string | null&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;Same signature as Angular's DatePipe — format defaults to DatePipe's own default.&#x22;,\n  },\n  fileSize: {\n    type: &#x22;{ key: string; value: number }&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;No arguments. key is one of memorySize.{bytes,kb,mb,gb,tb,pb} — your app must translate it.&#x22;,\n  },\n  operator: {\n    type: &#x22;string&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;No arguments beyond the LegacyFilter itself.&#x22;,\n  },\n  localizedNumber: {\n    type: &#x22;string&#x22;,\n    description: &#x22;No arguments. Groups the digits as the interface language does; null and undefined give 0.&#x22;,\n  },\n}\"\n/>"],[1769,"<TypeTable\n  type=\"{\n  &#x22;initialize()&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(): Promise<void>&#x22;, description: &#x22;Fetches user settings from the backend and patches the store. Call once, typically at bootstrap.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;reset()&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(): Promise<void>&#x22;, description: &#x22;Resets the store to its initial (empty) state.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;bookmark(article, queryName?)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(article: Article, queryName?: string): Promise<void>&#x22;, description: &#x22;Adds an article to bookmarks, if not already bookmarked.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;unbookmark(id)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(id: string): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;isBookmarked(article)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(article: Partial<Article>): boolean&#x22; },\n  &#x22;toggleBookmark(article)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(article: Article): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;addCurrentSearch(queryParams)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(queryParams: QueryParams): Promise<void>&#x22;, description: &#x22;Adds the current search to recent searches.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;deleteRecentSearch(index)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(index: number): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;updateSavedSearches(list)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(list: UserSettings['savedSearches']): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;deleteSavedSearch(index)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(index: number): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;createBasket(basket)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(basket: Basket): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;updateBasket(basket, index)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(basket: Basket, index: number): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;addToBasket(name, ids)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(name: string, ids: string | string[]): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;removeFromBasket(name, ids)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(name: string, ids: string | string[]): Promise<void>&#x22;, description: &#x22;Takes the ids out of the collection (audited as Basket_RemoveDoc). Used by the detail of the collections manager.&#x22; },\n  &#x22;deleteBasket(index)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(index: number): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;createAlert(alert)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(alert: Alert): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;updateAlert(alert, index)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(alert: Alert, index: number): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;deleteAlert(index)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(index: number): Promise<void>&#x22; },\n  &#x22;updateLanguage(language)&#x22;: { type: &#x22;(language: UserSettings['language']): Promise<void>&#x22;, description: &#x22;Also applied through Transloco by the callers that ex