rsion`, defaulting to the first/primary option every time\nit (re)mounts — which is exactly what happens if it sits inside a conditionally-rendered branch that gets torn\ndown and rebuilt by an unrelated reactive change (nested inside `<preview-actions>` inside\n`<preview-content>`'s own conditional branch, for instance). Pass the host's current conversion back as\n`[activeConversion]` so the dropdown re-syncs instead of resetting."],[1286,"This directive has no inputs of its own and no outputs — it exists purely to expose `template` to a querying\nparent."],[1317,"This directive has no outputs — opening happens as a side effect through the injected `PreviewService`."],[1450,"This directive has one output, `selected` (`OutputEmitterRef<void>`), emitted only when `strategy=\"emit\"`."],[327,"This interceptor reads the configuration and session of the **default** client. A client provided at the root\nholds its own snapshot of the configuration, so the `backendUrl` it resolves and the impersonation state it\nrecords never reach this code — keep the application root on the default client if you need this interceptor\nto see it."],[877,"This is the distribution-bounds trap: two chips can share the same **value** (two ends of a bucket), so a\nvalue-keyed removal drops both. `<applied-filters>` itself keys removal on the chip's **leaf**, not its value,\nso this should not happen inside the component — but if you reconcile your own selection state afterward,\nmake sure you compare with `filterKey`, not the chip's `value`."],[628,"This library ships no date adapter, and Chart.js only validates that at runtime, not at compile time. Every\nchart in this entry point uses a `category` axis instead — see\nAggregation Timeline for the time-series case."],[313,"This only applies in `credentials` mode, and only when `editablePartition` is set on the principal — an\naccount on a partition that does not manage passwords locally (SSO, an external identity provider) has\nnothing to expire from this guard's point of view."],[339,"This page describes how `@sinequa/atomic-angular` **orchestrates** authentication — how the pieces fit\ntogether and how every flow is handled. It is the Angular-side companion to the core state machine\ndocumented by `@sinequa/atomic` (detection, `login()` resolution, the OAuth/SAML redirect loop guard, error\nenvelopes) — this page never repeats that part, only what Angular adds on top of it."],[22,"This walks through the whole path from an empty Angular application to a first authenticated screen showing\nsearch results and a facet. Each step links to the page that covers it in full — this tutorial only shows the\nminimum needed to reach the next step."],[1067,"Those packages read `globalConfig.backendUrl`/`.app`/`.userOverride*` directly — they know nothing of\n`ATOMIC_CLIENT`. A client created by `provideAtomicClient()` keeps a *snapshot* of the configuration it was\nhanded, so `initializeConfig()` resolves `backendUrl` on that client alone, and the other packages go on\nbuilding requests against `undefined`. Nothing warns you — the rest of the application keeps working, since\nit goes through the client."],[1178,"Three Angular HttpInterceptorFn covering requests the atomic client never sees — the ui-language body field, audit metadata, and 401 recovery on the HttpClient channel."],[130,"Three conditions must all hold: the host passes `[allowAI]=\"true\"` (it is `false` by default, because the lib\ncannot know whether the app embeds an assistant), `AppStore.isAssistantAllowed()` is true for the instance\n(a `defaultValues.service_id` is configured), and that instance has `modeSettings.enabledUserInput` set. The\ninstance is resolved as `<app-name>-search-results-assistant` (or plain `search-results-assistant`, depending on\n`appFeatures.features.assistant.usePrefixName`). Check them in that order before assuming the button is broken."],[1861,"Three directives working together: `overflowManager` on the container, `overflowItem` on each candidate item,\nand `overflowStop` on the trigger whose space must always stay clear (a \"+N more\" button, typically)."],[1302,"Three dots that wave one after the other — a quiet loader for a control or a panel that is waiting for its content, used by the preview and by the Load more button of the facets."],[102,"Three independent ways a user keeps track of things across sessions: a single bookmarked document, a whole\nsaved (or automatically recorded) search, and — for the moment several documents are selected at once — a\nfloating action bar."],[2703,"Three things happen once, in order: the dialog opens through one of three methods, its content mounts, and\nclosing it resolves whatever awaited the call."],[594,"Three unrelated ways the application and the user talk to each other outside the current screen: a scheduled\nalert the user set up themselves, an automatic toast when a request fails, and a button for the user to send\nfeedback about the results."],[1295,"Three waving dots: the loader of the preview and of the Load more button of the facets."],[2075,"Tick workspaces from a menu"],[1991,"Tick workspaces from a menu: search, select all, categories."],[489,"To make the bookmark button of a result write to the collections instead, set `target`:"],[598,"Toast interceptor"],[785,"today"],[2163,"Toggle"],[335,"Token expiry and 401 re-authentication"],[2893,"Tooltip"],[2641,"Track the `@for` by the item itself: the elements are moved, not recreated, which is what lets them slide and keep\nthe keyboard focus. The element being dragged carries `data-dragging` — style it as the placeholder of the slot the\nitem will land in (`data-dragging:border-dashed`, and `data-dragging:*:invisible` to empty it)."],[2991,"Trailing badge and disabled item"],[229,"translated text"],[1048,"Translations come from the `quick-filters` scope (`en`, `fr`, `de`)."],[256,"Translations come from the `search-box` scope (`en`, `fr`, `de`)."],[1258,"Transloco answers in its default language until the application applies the user's one, typically once their\nsettings are loaded, and nothing signals that moment. `startupGraceMs` holds a warning that is already due at start-up\nback until then. Raise it if your settings take longer to load; later warnings are not affected."],[1986,"Transloco, with the `document-workspaces` translation scope the package ships, and `provideTranslocoMessageformat()`:\nthe plurals (\"3 people have access\") are written in ICU."],[2478,"Tree rows"],[1928,"Turn a corrected result's didYouMean property into the right message."],[596,"Turn a failed HttpClient request into a toast, for the statuses worth telling the user about."],[1798,"Turn a result's spelling correction into the right message — the engine corrected the query, expanded it, or only suggests a correction — and let the user switch between the two spellings."],[2756,"Turn on `topLayer`. A `fixed` panel escapes an ancestor's `overflow`, but a modal `<dialog>` and a `transform`ed ancestor\nstill clip or re-anchor it; the top layer is above all of them."],[1843,"Turn the engine's spelling correction into the right message — corrected, expanded, or merely suggested."],[2632,"Turning it off"],[2661,"Two actions, one per direction"],[1109,"Two backends on the same screen is a component-tree question, not a routing one — `provideAtomicScope()` also\nworks in a component's `providers`."],[854,"Two backends sharing one screen"],[1096,"Two backends side by side"],[259,"Two components of `@sinequa/atomic-angular` deal with people. `<principal-picker>` searches the directory and\nhands back the people picked. `<manage-access>` builds on it: a whole \"Manage access\" block — owners, readers, a\npublic switch — that edits two plain lists of ids. Neither knows what it is attached to: a workspace, an agent, a\nskill or an upload can use them the same way."],[1874,"Two components that each render one property of an `Article`, nothing more — no interaction, no state